<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="Ch34.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 34.3</span>. </span><span id="Ch34.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Speculative learned topology for the belief space <math id="Ch34.F3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>. Instead of being flat or Euclidean, structural learning could induce a nontrivial manifold topology (e.g., a torus) to optimize semantic organization.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="Ch34.S5" class="ltx_section">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">34.5. </span>Relation to Representation Mode (<math id="Ch34.S5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>)</h4>

<div id="Ch34.S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch34.S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is crucial to reiterate that the specific mechanisms viable for learning semantic structures are heavily dependent on the chosen representation mode <math id="Ch34.S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> defined in the agent’s parameter vector <math id="Ch34.S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> (Chapter <a href="#Ch8" title="Chapter 8 Parameterized Semantic Architectures ‣ Part II Semantic Foundations: The Semantic Substrate ‣ Theoretical Foundations for Semantic Cognition in Artificial Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>).</p>
<ul id="Ch34.S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="Ch34.S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ch34.S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch34.S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Embedding-based <math id="Ch34.S5.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> lends itself naturally to metric learning, clustering, and dimensionality reduction using techniques from deep learning.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ch34.S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ch34.S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch34.S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Symbolic <math id="Ch34.S5.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> requires different techniques, such as inductive logic programming, grammar induction, or symbolic clustering methods.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ch34.S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ch34.S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch34.S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Graph-based <math id="Ch34.S5.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> might leverage graph neural networks for learning node embeddings (affecting <math id="Ch34.S5.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>) or community detection algorithms for identifying sectors (<math id="Ch34.S5.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ch34.S5.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ch34.S5.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch34.S5.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Hybrid <math id="Ch34.S5.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> necessitates combining techniques suitable for each component.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="Ch34.S5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The design choice of <math id="Ch34.S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> therefore strongly constrains the pathways available for structural learning within <math id="Ch34.S5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
