<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="Ch35.F5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 35.5</span>. </span><span id="Ch35.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Learning-augmented decay and annihilation process. Belief fragments <math id="Ch35.F5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> undergo anchoring decay over time. When the anchoring strength <math id="Ch35.F5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> drops below a threshold <math id="Ch35.F5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, the agent evaluates whether to allow natural decay via <math id="Ch35.F5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> or trigger forced annihilation <math id="Ch35.F5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, with learned modules optionally adapting decay profiles and annihilation policies.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="Ch35.S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="Ch35.S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="Ch35.S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ch35.S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch35.S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch35.S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Tuning Decay Parameters (<math id="Ch35.S5.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{profile}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{profile}</annotation></semantics></math>):</span> The specific rates or thresholds governing decay in different sectors or for beliefs with different properties might be learnable parameters within <math id="Ch35.S5.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, optimized based on overall performance (e.g., balancing memory retention vs. avoiding clutter). RL could be used to tune these based on long-term task success.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ch35.S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ch35.S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch35.S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch35.S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Learning Anchoring Dynamics (<math id="Ch35.S5.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>):</span> The rules governing how anchoring <math id="Ch35.S5.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> changes based on usage (via <math id="Ch35.S5.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Ch35.S5.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>) or perceived importance could be learned, perhaps by optimizing the anchoring updates to maximize the utility of retained information.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ch35.S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ch35.S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch35.S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch35.S5.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Learning Annihilation Conditions (<math id="Ch35.S5.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>):</span> The agent might learn more nuanced conditions under which Annihilation <math id="Ch35.S5.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> (total or sectoral <math id="Ch35.S5.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>) is triggered, beyond simple contradiction detection, perhaps based on persistent negative impact on coherence <math id="Ch35.S5.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> or goal achievement. This could involve learning a classifier or policy based on historical data where annihilation proved beneficial or detrimental.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="Ch35.S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Learning in this context allows the agent to optimize its "cognitive metabolism"—how it maintains useful information and prunes the irrelevant or harmful.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Ch35.S6" class="ltx_section">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">35.6. </span>Conclusion: Towards Adaptive Cognitive Processes</h4>

<div id="Ch35.S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch35.S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This chapter has argued that the core cognitive operators (<math id="Ch35.S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A,M,\Lambda,V,R,Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A,M,\Lambda,V,R,Q</annotation></semantics></math>, and potentially aspects of <math id="Ch35.S6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{t},K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{t},K</annotation></semantics></math>) within the Semantic Manifold framework need not be static, predefined functions. By leveraging techniques from machine learning—including representation learning, sequence modeling, reinforcement learning, and supervised fine-tuning—agents can potentially learn or adapt these crucial processes based on their experience.</p>
</div>
<div id="Ch35.S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch35.S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Learning effective operators allows an agent to tailor its cognitive dynamics to its specific environment, tasks, and internal architecture (<math id="Ch35.S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>). It enables the development of sophisticated abstraction hierarchies, context-sensitive assimilation strategies, efficient memory access, and optimized decay/pruning mechanisms. Combined with the structural learning discussed in Chapter <a href="#Ch34" title="Chapter 34 Learning Semantic Structures ‣ Part IX Learning and Adaptation ‣ Theoretical Foundations for Semantic Cognition in Artificial Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">34</span></a> and the policy learning in Chapter <a href="#Ch36" title="Chapter 36 Learning Regulatory Policies ‣ Part IX Learning and Adaptation ‣ Theoretical Foundations for Semantic Cognition in Artificial Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">36</span></a>, learning cognitive operators is essential for creating agents that not only possess structured beliefs but can also process and manipulate those beliefs in increasingly adaptive and intelligent ways.</p>
</div>
<section id="Ch35.S6.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Chapter Summary</h5>

<div id="Ch35.S6.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch35.S6.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following the discussion on learning semantic structures, this chapter focuses on the learnability and adaptability of the core cognitive operators themselves within the Semantic Manifold framework. It argues that operators such as Abstraction (<math id="Ch35.S6.SSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>), Elaboration (<math id="Ch35.S6.SSx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>), Assimilation (<math id="Ch35.S6.SSx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>), Meta-Assimilation (<math id="Ch35.S6.SSx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>), Retrieval (<math id="Ch35.S6.SSx1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>), and Querying (<math id="Ch35.S6.SSx1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>), along with potentially aspects of Nullification (<math id="Ch35.S6.SSx1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>) and Annihilation (<math id="Ch35.S6.SSx1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>), need not be entirely fixed but can be refined through learning. Depending on the chosen representation mode (<math id="Ch35.S6.SSx1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>) and architecture (<math id="Ch35.S6.SSx1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>), various machine learning techniques could be employed. Examples include using representation learning or summarization techniques for <math id="Ch35.S6.SSx1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Ch35.S6.SSx1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>; reinforcement learning or fine-tuning generative models for aspects of <math id="Ch35.S6.SSx1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Ch35.S6.SSx1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> (like coherence checking or conflict revision); and learning effective query formulation (<math id="Ch35.S6.SSx1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>) and retrieval (<math id="Ch35.S6.SSx1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>) strategies based on task success. Learning decay parameters (<math id="Ch35.S6.SSx1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>) or annihilation triggers (<math id="Ch35.S6.SSx1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>) might also be possible. This allows the agent to tailor its fundamental cognitive processes—how it integrates information, navigates abstraction levels, accesses memory, and manages belief persistence—based on experience, leading to more adaptive and efficient cognition.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Ch36" class="ltx_chapter">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_chapter"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_chapter">Chapter 36 </span>Learning Regulatory Policies</h3>

<section id="Ch36.S1" class="ltx_section">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">36.1. </span>Introduction: Acquiring Cognitive Control</h4>

<div id="Ch36.S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch36.S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The preceding chapters in this Part explored the learning of semantic structures (Chapter <a href="#Ch34" title="Chapter 34 Learning Semantic Structures ‣ Part IX Learning and Adaptation ‣ Theoretical Foundations for Semantic Cognition in Artificial Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">34</span></a>) and core cognitive operators (Chapter <a href="#Ch35" title="Chapter 35 Learning Cognitive Operators ‣ Part IX Learning and Adaptation ‣ Theoretical Foundations for Semantic Cognition in Artificial Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">35</span></a>). However, possessing effective representations and operators is only part of the equation for adaptive intelligence. An agent must also possess effective control policies to deploy these resources appropriately—regulating its internal state, managing limited resources like semantic effort (<math id="Ch36.S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>), and guiding its cognitive trajectory <math id="Ch36.S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(t)</annotation></semantics></math> towards its goals while maintaining coherence (<math id="Ch36.S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>) and identity (<math id="Ch36.S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="Ch36.S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch36.S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Part <a href="#Pt6" title="Part VI Regulation and Control: Compass, Gauge, Identity ‣ Theoretical Foundations for Semantic Cognition in Artificial Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI</span></a> introduced regulatory concepts like Orientation and Identity, while Part <a href="#Pt8" title="Part VIII Meta-Cognition and Recursive Regulation ‣ Theoretical Foundations for Semantic Cognition in Artificial Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VIII</span></a> detailed meta-cognitive monitoring (<math id="Ch36.S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>) and resource management (<math id="Ch36.S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda,\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda,\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>) culminating in effort allocation policies (<math id="Ch36.S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{effort}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{effort}</annotation></semantics></math>, Chapter <a href="#Ch33" title="Chapter 33 Semantic Focus and Effort Allocation Policies ‣ Part VIII Meta-Cognition and Recursive Regulation ‣ Theoretical Foundations for Semantic Cognition in Artificial Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">33</span></a>). This chapter addresses the critical question: how are these regulatory and allocation policies themselves acquired or refined?</p>
</div>
<div id="Ch36.S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch36.S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Relying solely on predefined, fixed control policies limits an agent’s adaptability. True cognitive flexibility requires the ability to learn effective strategies for self-regulation and resource management based on experience. We explore potential mechanisms, primarily drawing from reinforcement learning (RL) and related fields, by which an agent might learn optimal policies like <math id="Ch36.S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{effort}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{effort}</annotation></semantics></math> and the broader meta-control policy <math id="Ch36.S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{regulate}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{regulate}</annotation></semantics></math> that governs overall cognitive strategy.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Ch36.F1" class="ltx_figure"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="Ch36.F1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="204.11" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 520.96 204.11" width="520.96"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,204.11) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(254.47,0) translate(0,179.93)"><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 62.56 23.62 L -62.56 23.62 C -67.15 23.62 -70.87 19.91 -70.87 15.32 L -70.87 -15.32 C -70.87 -19.91 -67.15 -23.62 -62.56 -23.62 L 62.56 -23.62 C 67.15 -23.62 70.87 -19.91 70.87 -15.32 L 70.87 15.32 C 70.87 19.91 67.15 23.62 62.56 23.62 Z M -70.87 -23.62"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.8pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -56.99 -6.49)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 19.89)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 8.65)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_r" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:8.96em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="11.07" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.65)" width="114.69"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="Ch36.F1.pic1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Meta-Control Policy</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 16.43)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_r" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 32.39 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.84em;--ltx-fo-height:0.42em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.27em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="8.82" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 5.36)" width="49.2"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="Ch36.F1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{regulate}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">π</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{regulate}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -119.65 -44.41 L -245.61 -44.41 C -250.2 -44.41 -253.92 -48.13 -253.92 -52.72 L -253.92 -83.36 C -253.92 -87.94 -250.2 -91.66 -245.61 -91.66 L -119.65 -91.66 C -115.06 -91.66 -111.34 -87.94 -111.34 -83.36 L -111.34 -52.72 C -111.34 -48.13 -115.06 -44.41 -119.65 -44.41 Z M -253.92 -91.66"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.8pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -249.3 -74.52)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 19.89)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 8.65)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_r" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:10.42em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="11.07" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.65)" width="133.35"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="Ch36.F1.pic1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Effort Allocation Policy</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 16.43)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_r" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 46.45 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.16em;--ltx-fo-height:0.42em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.27em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="8.82" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 5.36)" width="40.46"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="Ch36.F1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{effort}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">π</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{effort}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 257.63 -44.41 L 119.65 -44.41 C 115.06 -44.41 111.34 -48.13 111.34 -52.72 L 111.34 -83.36 C 111.34 -87.94 115.06 -91.66 119.65 -91.66 L 257.63 -91.66 C 262.22 -91.66 265.94 -87.94 265.94 -83.36 L 265.94 -52.72 C 265.94 -48.13 262.22 -44.41 257.63 -44.41 Z M 111.34 -91.66"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.8pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 115.96 -76.68)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 23.52)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 8.65)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_r" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:11.36em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="11.07" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.65)" width="145.37"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="Ch36.F1.pic1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Other Regulatory Actions</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 20.41)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_r" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0.09 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:8.73em;--ltx-fo-height:0.73em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.24em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="12.45" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.34)" width="111.72"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="Ch36.F1.pic1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(e.g., </span><math id="Ch36.F1.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K,N_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">K</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">N</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K,N_{t}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Ch36.F1.pic1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, Mode Shifts)</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -120.07 -132.14 L -245.19 -132.14 C -249.78 -132.14 -253.5 -135.85 -253.5 -140.44 L -253.5 -171.08 C -253.5 -175.66 -249.78 -179.38 -245.19 -179.38 L -120.07 -179.38 C -115.48 -179.38 -111.76 -175.66 -111.76 -171.08 L -111.76 -140.44 C -111.76 -135.85 -115.48 -132.14 -120.07 -132.14 Z M -253.5 -179.38"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.8pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -242.24 -164.34)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 23.38)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 8.51)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_r" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:9.31em;--ltx-fo-height:0.66em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="10.93" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.51)" width="119.21"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="Ch36.F1.pic1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Cognitive Operations</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 20.27)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_r" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 17.35 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.78em;--ltx-fo-height:0.73em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.24em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="12.45" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.34)" width="9.96"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="Ch36.F1.pic1.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(</span><math id="Ch36.F1.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A,M,R,Q,\dots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">A</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">M</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">R</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">Q</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A,M,R,Q,\dots</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Ch36.F1.pic1.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">)</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 251.2 -132.14 L 126.08 -132.14 C 121.49 -132.14 117.77 -135.85 117.77 -140.44 L 117.77 -171.08 C 117.77 -175.66 121.49 -179.38 126.08 -179.38 L 251.2 -179.38 C 255.79 -179.38 259.51 -175.66 259.51 -171.08 L 259.51 -140.44 C 259.51 -135.85 255.79 -132.14 251.2 -132.14 Z M 117.77 -179.38"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.8pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 139.92 -163.4)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 21.51)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 8.51)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_r" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:7.67em;--ltx-fo-height:0.66em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="8.51" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.51)" width="98.16"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="Ch36.F1.pic1.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Sector Activation</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 18.4)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_r" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 31.93 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.78em;--ltx-fo-height:0.73em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.24em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="12.45" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.34)" width="9.96"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="Ch36.F1.pic1.10" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(</span><math id="Ch36.F1.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\alpha}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">α</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\alpha}(t)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Ch36.F1.pic1.11" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">)</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -64.9 -24.18 L -112.19 -41.78"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" transform="matrix(-0.93716 -0.34889 0.34889 -0.93716 -109.22 -40.67)"><path d="M 7.66 0 L 1.79 2.18 L 3.45 0 L 1.79 -2.18 Z"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 67.04 -24.18 L 116.04 -41.84"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" transform="matrix(0.94077 -0.33907 0.33907 0.94077 113.06 -40.76)"><path d="M 7.66 0 L 1.79 2.18 L 3.45 0 L 1.79 -2.18 Z"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -182.63 -92.21 L -182.63 -125.62"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 -182.63 -122.45)"><path d="M 7.66 0 L 1.79 2.18 L 3.45 0 L 1.79 -2.18 Z"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 188.64 -92.21 L 188.64 -125.62"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 188.64 -122.45)"><path d="M 7.66 0 L 1.79 2.18 L 3.45 0 L 1.79 -2.18 Z"></path></g></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="Ch36.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 36.1</span>. </span><span id="Ch36.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Hierarchical structure of learned regulatory policies. The meta-control policy <math id="Ch36.F1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{regulate}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{regulate}</annotation></semantics></math> decides when and how to intervene in cognitive processes. It may trigger effort reallocation via <math id="Ch36.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{effort}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{effort}</annotation></semantics></math> or other regulatory actions such as sectoral nullification (<math id="Ch36.F1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>) or annihilation (<math id="Ch36.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>). These influence the underlying cognitive operations and sector activations.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="Ch36.S2" class="ltx_section">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">36.2. </span>Learning Effort Allocation Policies (<math id="Ch36.S2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{effort}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>effort</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{effort}}</annotation></semantics></math>)</h4>

<div id="Ch36.S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch36.S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Chapter <a href="#Ch33" title="Chapter 33 Semantic Focus and Effort Allocation Policies ‣ Part VIII Meta-Cognition and Recursive Regulation ‣ Theoretical Foundations for Semantic Cognition in Artificial Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">33</span></a> defined the effort allocation policy <math id="Ch36.S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{effort}:\Phi\times\text{Context}\rightarrow\text{Allocation}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mtext>Context</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mtext>Allocation</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{effort}:\Phi\times\text{Context}\rightarrow\text{Allocation}</annotation></semantics></math> which distributes the available effort resource <math id="Ch36.S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{total}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{total}</annotation></semantics></math> across competing cognitive demands. Learning an effective <math id="Ch36.S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{effort}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{effort}</annotation></semantics></math> is crucial for efficient focus and attention management. Reinforcement Learning provides a natural framework:</p>
</div>
<div id="Ch36.S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="Ch36.S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="Ch36.S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ch36.S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch36.S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch36.S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">State Representation:</span> The RL state (<math id="Ch36.S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>) could incorporate features extracted from the current belief state <math id="Ch36.S2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>, particularly meta-cognitive information from <math id="Ch36.S2.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{refl}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{refl}</annotation></semantics></math> (outputs of <math id="Ch36.S2.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>), such as current load <math id="Ch36.S2.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, coherence <math id="Ch36.S2.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>, goal priorities, trajectory deviation <math id="Ch36.S2.I1.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, remaining capacity <math id="Ch36.S2.I1.i1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{total}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{total}</annotation></semantics></math>, etc.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ch36.S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ch36.S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch36.S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch36.S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Action Space:</span> The actions (<math id="Ch36.S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>) correspond to different ways of allocating effort <math id="Ch36.S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\epsilon}=(\epsilon_{1},\dots,\epsilon_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\epsilon}=(\epsilon_{1},\dots,\epsilon_{m})</annotation></semantics></math> across targets (operators, sectors, tasks), subject to <math id="Ch36.S2.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum\epsilon_{i}\leq E_{total}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum\epsilon_{i}\leq E_{total}</annotation></semantics></math>. This might involve selecting high-level allocation strategies (e.g., "focus on planning," "monitor environment," "restore coherence").</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ch36.S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ch36.S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch36.S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch36.S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reward Signal (<math id="Ch36.S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(s,a,s^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(s,a,s^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>):</span> Defining an effective reward signal is critical for learning useful policies. The reward function could be based on:</p>
<ul id="Ch36.S2.I1.i3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="Ch36.S2.I1.i3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="Ch36.S2.I1.i3.I1.i1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="Ch36.S2.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch36.S2.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch36.S2.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Internal Metrics:</span> Providing rewards for desirable internal states resulting from an effort allocation. Examples: +1 for maintaining high coherence <math id="Ch36.S2.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>, +1 for staying aligned with orientation axes (low <math id="Ch36.S2.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>), -1 for exceeding load thresholds <math id="Ch36.S2.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, +1 for making progress towards goals represented in <math id="Ch36.S2.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{plan}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{plan}</annotation></semantics></math>. Designing these internal rewards requires careful consideration to ensure they align with overall desired cognitive function.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ch36.S2.I1.i3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="Ch36.S2.I1.i3.I1.i2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="Ch36.S2.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch36.S2.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch36.S2.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">External Task Performance:</span> More directly, rewards can be based on successful completion of external tasks that depend on effective internal resource management. An allocation policy leading to faster or more accurate task completion receives higher reward.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ch36.S2.I1.i3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="Ch36.S2.I1.i3.I1.i3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="Ch36.S2.I1.i3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch36.S2.I1.i3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch36.S2.I1.i3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Efficiency Metrics:</span> Rewards could incorporate efficiency, e.g., rewarding goal achievement while penalizing excessive effort <math id="Ch36.S2.I1.i3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> expenditure, encouraging the agent to learn resource-frugal strategies.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
<li id="Ch36.S2.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ch36.S2.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch36.S2.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch36.S2.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Learning Algorithms:</span> Standard RL algorithms (Q-learning, policy gradients, actor-critic methods) could be adapted to learn the mapping from state features to optimal effort allocation actions, using the defined reward signals.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ch36.S2.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ch36.S2.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch36.S2.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch36.S2.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Imitation Learning:</span> <math id="Ch36.S2.I1.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{effort}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{effort}</annotation></semantics></math> could also be learned by imitating the allocation patterns of successful demonstrators (human or other AI), potentially bootstrapping the RL process.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ch36.S2.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ch36.S2.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch36.S2.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch36.S2.I1.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Meta-Learning:</span> Learning algorithms could adapt the parameters of <math id="Ch36.S2.I1.i6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{effort}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{effort}</annotation></semantics></math> itself based on performance across different types of tasks or contexts (learning how to allocate effort differently in different situations).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="Ch36.S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Learning <math id="Ch36.S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{effort}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{effort}</annotation></semantics></math> allows the agent to develop sophisticated, context-sensitive attentional control strategies beyond simple heuristics, optimized according to specified internal or external objectives captured in the reward function.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Ch36.S3" class="ltx_section">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">36.3. </span>Learning Meta-Control Policies (<math id="Ch36.S3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{regulate}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>regulate</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{regulate}}</annotation></semantics></math>)</h4>

<div id="Ch36.S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch36.S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond allocating effort, the agent employs broader meta-control policies (<math id="Ch36.S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{regulate}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{regulate}</annotation></semantics></math>) to manage its overall cognitive state and strategy. These policies decide when to trigger specific regulatory actions based on introspective inputs assimilated via <math id="Ch36.S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. Learning <math id="Ch36.S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{regulate}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{regulate}</annotation></semantics></math> enables adaptive self-governance.</p>
</div>
<div id="Ch36.S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="Ch36.S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="Ch36.S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ch36.S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch36.S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch36.S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Learning Triggers:</span> RL or supervised learning could train the agent to recognize internal states (specific patterns of <math id="Ch36.S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa,\lambda,\theta,\vec{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa,\lambda,\theta,\vec{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math> divergence reported by <math id="Ch36.S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>) that warrant intervention. The state representation for the learning algorithm would heavily rely on meta-beliefs in <math id="Ch36.S3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{refl}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{refl}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ch36.S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ch36.S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch36.S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch36.S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Learning Action Selection:</span> Given a problematic state detected via introspection, <math id="Ch36.S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{regulate}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{regulate}</annotation></semantics></math> must select the appropriate corrective action. Learning could determine whether it’s better to:</p>
<ul id="Ch36.S3.I1.i2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="Ch36.S3.I1.i2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="Ch36.S3.I1.i2.I1.i1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="Ch36.S3.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch36.S3.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Initiate re-orientation (Chapter <a href="#Ch22" title="Chapter 22 Semantic Orientation ‣ Part VI Regulation and Control: Compass, Gauge, Identity ‣ Theoretical Foundations for Semantic Cognition in Artificial Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ch36.S3.I1.i2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="Ch36.S3.I1.i2.I1.i2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="Ch36.S3.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch36.S3.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Trigger Annihilation (<math id="Ch36.S3.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, Chapter <a href="#Ch15" title="Chapter 15 Annihilation ‣ Part IV Epistemic Dynamics: Evolution of Thought ‣ Theoretical Foundations for Semantic Cognition in Artificial Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>) of conflicting subsystems (<math id="Ch36.S3.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ch36.S3.I1.i2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="Ch36.S3.I1.i2.I1.i3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="Ch36.S3.I1.i2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch36.S3.I1.i2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Apply targeted Nullification (<math id="Ch36.S3.I1.i2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, Chapter <a href="#Ch14" title="Chapter 14 Nullification ‣ Part IV Epistemic Dynamics: Evolution of Thought ‣ Theoretical Foundations for Semantic Cognition in Artificial Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) to prune irrelevant data (<math id="Ch36.S3.I1.i2.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{\Sigma}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{\Sigma}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ch36.S3.I1.i2.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="Ch36.S3.I1.i2.I1.i4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="Ch36.S3.I1.i2.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch36.S3.I1.i2.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Engage in deep reflection (<math id="Ch36.S3.I1.i2.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> loop) to resolve inconsistencies.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ch36.S3.I1.i2.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="Ch36.S3.I1.i2.I1.i5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="Ch36.S3.I1.i2.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch36.S3.I1.i2.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Shift cognitive mode (adjust sector activations <math id="Ch36.S3.I1.i2.I1.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\alpha}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\alpha}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ch36.S3.I1.i2.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="Ch36.S3.I1.i2.I1.i6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="Ch36.S3.I1.i2.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch36.S3.I1.i2.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Adjust parameters of object-level operators (e.g., make <math id="Ch36.S3.I1.i2.I1.i6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> more conservative).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="Ch36.S3.I1.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The RL reward signal would reflect the effectiveness of the chosen regulatory action in restoring desired state properties (e.g., reward for increase in <math id="Ch36.S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>, decrease in <math id="Ch36.S3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, successful goal completion following intervention).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ch36.S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ch36.S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch36.S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch36.S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Relation to <math id="Ch36.S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{effort}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{effort}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span> <math id="Ch36.S3.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{regulate}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{regulate}</annotation></semantics></math> likely operates at a higher level, potentially setting goals or constraints (like "prioritize coherence restoration") for the more fine-grained allocation policy <math id="Ch36.S3.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{effort}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{effort}</annotation></semantics></math>. Learning must coordinate these levels, perhaps through hierarchical RL.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
