</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Formal Operator Definitions</h3>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This appendix provides more detailed, semi-formal definitions or algorithmic sketches for the core cognitive operators introduced in the main text. These definitions aim to clarify the intended function and inputs/outputs of each operator. Note that concrete implementations will depend heavily on the specific architectural parameters <math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, particularly the representation mode <math id="A2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.S1" class="ltx_section">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">B.1. </span>Assimilation (<math id="A2.S1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>)</h4>

<div id="A2.S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Assimilation operator integrates object-level input <math id="A2.S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{input}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{input}</annotation></semantics></math> into the current state <math id="A2.S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{current}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{current}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
<table id="A2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A:\Phi\times\Phi_{input}\rightarrow\Phi" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A:\Phi\times\Phi_{input}\rightarrow\Phi</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="A2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{new}=A(\phi_{current},\phi_{input})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{new}=A(\phi_{current},\phi_{input})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A general algorithm sketch involves stages:</p>
<ol id="A2.S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A2.S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="A2.S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Filtering/Salience (Optional):</span> <math id="A2.S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{\prime}_{input}=\text{Filter}(\phi_{input},\phi_{current})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Filter</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{\prime}_{input}=\text{Filter}(\phi_{input},\phi_{current})</annotation></semantics></math> based on relevance/salience.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="A2.S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conflict Detection:</span> Identify set <math id="A2.S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="C=\{(\varphi_{i},\varphi_{j})\mid\varphi_{i}\in\phi_{current},\varphi_{j}\in\phi^{\prime}_{input},\text{Contradict}(\varphi_{i},\varphi_{j})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mtext>Contradict</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=\{(\varphi_{i},\varphi_{j})\mid\varphi_{i}\in\phi_{current},\varphi_{j}\in\phi^{\prime}_{input},\text{Contradict}(\varphi_{i},\varphi_{j})\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="A2.S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Revision (if <math id="A2.S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>, corresponds to <math id="A2.S1.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{corr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{corr}</annotation></semantics></math>):</span></p>
<ul id="A2.S1.I1.i3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.S1.I1.i3.I0.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.S1.I1.i3.I0.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S1.I1.i3.I0.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Determine beliefs to revise/retract from <math id="A2.S1.I1.i3.I0.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{current}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{current}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="A2.S1.I1.i3.I0.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{retract}=R_{\text{rev/ret}}(\phi_{current},\phi^{\prime}_{input},C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>rev/ret</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{retract}=R_{\text{rev/ret}}(\phi_{current},\phi^{\prime}_{input},C)</annotation></semantics></math> (based on anchoring <math id="A2.S1.I1.i3.I0.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, coherence <math id="A2.S1.I1.i3.I0.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>, etc.).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.S1.I1.i3.I0.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.S1.I1.i3.I0.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S1.I1.i3.I0.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Potentially modify input: <math id="A2.S1.I1.i3.I0.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{\prime\prime}_{input}=\text{ModifyInput}(\phi^{\prime}_{input},C,\phi_{retract})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>ModifyInput</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{\prime\prime}_{input}=\text{ModifyInput}(\phi^{\prime}_{input},C,\phi_{retract})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.S1.I1.i3.I0.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.S1.I1.i3.I0.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S1.I1.i3.I0.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Update current state: <math id="A2.S1.I1.i3.I0.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{revised}=\phi_{current}\setminus\phi_{retract}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{revised}=\phi_{current}\setminus\phi_{retract}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="A2.S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.S1.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Integration:</span> Combine revised state and input: <math id="A2.S1.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{merged}=\phi_{revised}\cup\phi^{\prime\prime}_{input}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∪</mo><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{merged}=\phi_{revised}\cup\phi^{\prime\prime}_{input}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.S1.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(5)</span> 
<div id="A2.S1.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S1.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.S1.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Elaboration (corresponds to <math id="A2.S1.I1.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{elab}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{elab}</annotation></semantics></math>):</span> Generate elaborations <math id="A2.S1.I1.i5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{elab}=\text{Elaborate}(\phi_{merged})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Elaborate</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{elab}=\text{Elaborate}(\phi_{merged})</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="A2.S1.I1.i5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{pre\_final}=\phi_{merged}\cup\phi_{elab}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{pre\_final}=\phi_{merged}\cup\phi_{elab}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.S1.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(6)</span> 
<div id="A2.S1.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S1.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.S1.I1.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Abstraction (corresponds to <math id="A2.S1.I1.i6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{abs}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{abs}</annotation></semantics></math>, optional):</span> Identify patterns in <math id="A2.S1.I1.i6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{pre\_final}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{pre\_final}</annotation></semantics></math> and generate abstractions <math id="A2.S1.I1.i6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{abs}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{abs}</annotation></semantics></math>. Potentially remove summarized beliefs. <math id="A2.S1.I1.i6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{final}=(\phi_{pre\_final}\setminus\phi_{summarized})\cup\phi_{abs}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{final}=(\phi_{pre\_final}\setminus\phi_{summarized})\cup\phi_{abs}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.S1.I1.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(7)</span> 
<div id="A2.S1.I1.i7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S1.I1.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.S1.I1.i7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Re-Anchoring:</span> Update anchoring scores <math id="A2.S1.I1.i7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for newly added or reinforced beliefs in <math id="A2.S1.I1.i7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{final}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{final}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.S1.I1.i8" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(8)</span> 
<div id="A2.S1.I1.i8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S1.I1.i8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.S1.I1.i8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Return:</span> <math id="A2.S1.I1.i8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{new}\leftarrow\phi_{final}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{new}\leftarrow\phi_{final}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="A2.S1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Specific subtypes emphasize certain steps (e.g., <math id="A2.S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{corr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{corr}</annotation></semantics></math> emphasizes step 3, <math id="A2.S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{elab}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{elab}</annotation></semantics></math> step 5).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.S2" class="ltx_section">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">B.2. </span>Nullification (<math id="A2.S2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>)</h4>

<div id="A2.S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Nullification operator models gradual decay towards <math id="A2.S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
<table id="A2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{t}:\Phi\rightarrow\Phi" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{t}:\Phi\rightarrow\Phi</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="A2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{t}=N_{t}(\phi_{0})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{t}=N_{t}(\phi_{0})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.S2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Implemented elementwise based on persistence <math id="A2.S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> and anchoring <math id="A2.S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<ol id="A2.S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A2.S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="A2.S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each <math id="A2.S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{i}\in\phi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{i}\in\phi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<ul id="A2.S2.I1.i1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.S2.I1.i1.I0.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.S2.I1.i1.I0.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S2.I1.i1.I0.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Retrieve current anchoring <math id="A2.S2.I1.i1.I0.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.S2.I1.i1.I0.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.S2.I1.i1.I0.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S2.I1.i1.I0.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Determine decay rate <math id="A2.S2.I1.i1.I0.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}=\lambda_{0}\cdot f(a_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}=\lambda_{0}\cdot f(a_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> (where <math id="A2.S2.I1.i1.I0.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> decreases with <math id="A2.S2.I1.i1.I0.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.S2.I1.i1.I0.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.S2.I1.i1.I0.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S2.I1.i1.I0.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Compute persistence at time <math id="A2.S2.I1.i1.I0.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> using a decay function, e.g., <math id="A2.S2.I1.i1.I0.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}(t)=d_{i}(0)\cdot e^{-\lambda_{i}t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}(t)=d_{i}(0)\cdot e^{-\lambda_{i}t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="A2.S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Define persistence threshold <math id="A2.S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="A2.S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Construct the nullified state: <math id="A2.S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{t}=\{\varphi_{i}\in\phi_{0}\mid d_{i}(t)&gt;\delta\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{t}=\{\varphi_{i}\in\phi_{0}\mid d_{i}(t)&gt;\delta\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.S2.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="A2.S2.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S2.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Update internal state to reflect new persistence values <math id="A2.S2.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.S2.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(5)</span> 
<div id="A2.S2.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S2.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Return <math id="A2.S2.I1.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="A2.S2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The choice of decay function <math id="A2.S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{-\lambda t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{-\lambda t}</annotation></semantics></math> or alternatives is part of <math id="A2.S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{profile}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{profile}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A2.S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.S3" class="ltx_section">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">B.3. </span>Annihilation (<math id="A2.S3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.S3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>)</h4>

<div id="A2.S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Annihilation models abrupt erasure.</p>
<ul id="A2.S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Total Annihilation (<math id="A2.S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>):</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K:\Phi\rightarrow\Omega" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K:\Phi\rightarrow\Omega</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="A2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(\phi)=\phi_{null}\quad\text{where }\phi_{null}\in\Omega" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>where </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(\phi)=\phi_{null}\quad\text{where }\phi_{null}\in\Omega</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.S3.I1.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Algorithm: Replace <math id="A2.S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> entirely with a pre-defined null state.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sectoral Annihilation (<math id="A2.S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>):</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\Sigma}:\Phi\rightarrow\Phi" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\Sigma}:\Phi\rightarrow\Phi</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="A2.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\Sigma}(\phi)=\phi\setminus\phi|_{\Sigma}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∖</mo><msub><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\Sigma}(\phi)=\phi\setminus\phi|_{\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.S3.I1.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Algorithm: Identify all belief fragments <math id="A2.S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> belonging to sector <math id="A2.S3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., <math id="A2.S3.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi|_{\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi|_{\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>) and remove them from <math id="A2.S3.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.S4" class="ltx_section">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">B.4. </span>Scaling (<math id="A2.S4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.S4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>)</h4>

<div id="A2.S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These operators manage abstraction levels <math id="A2.S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{(k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{(k)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
<ul id="A2.S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Abstraction (<math id="A2.S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>):</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda^{k}:\Phi^{(i)}\rightarrow\Phi^{(i+k)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda^{k}:\Phi^{(i)}\rightarrow\Phi^{(i+k)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.S4.I1.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Algorithm sketch (depends heavily on <math id="A2.S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>):</p>
<ol id="A2.S4.I1.i1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A2.S4.I1.i1.I0.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="A2.S4.I1.i1.I0.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S4.I1.i1.I0.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Input <math id="A2.S4.I1.i1.I0.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.S4.I1.i1.I0.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="A2.S4.I1.i1.I0.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S4.I1.i1.I0.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Apply compression/generalization function <math id="A2.S4.I1.i1.I0.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., autoencoder encoder, summarization model, clustering/prototype extraction).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.S4.I1.i1.I0.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="A2.S4.I1.i1.I0.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S4.I1.i1.I0.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Output <math id="A2.S4.I1.i1.I0.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{(j)}=f_{\Lambda}(\phi^{(i)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{(j)}=f_{\Lambda}(\phi^{(i)})</annotation></semantics></math>, ensuring result resides at level <math id="A2.S4.I1.i1.I0.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Elaboration (<math id="A2.S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>):</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{k}:\Phi^{(i)}\rightarrow\Phi^{(i-k)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{k}:\Phi^{(i)}\rightarrow\Phi^{(i-k)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.S4.I1.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Algorithm sketch:</p>
<ol id="A2.S4.I1.i2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A2.S4.I1.i2.I0.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="A2.S4.I1.i2.I0.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S4.I1.i2.I0.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Input <math id="A2.S4.I1.i2.I0.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{(j)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{(j)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.S4.I1.i2.I0.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="A2.S4.I1.i2.I0.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S4.I1.i2.I0.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Apply instantiation/decompression function <math id="A2.S4.I1.i2.I0.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{V}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., autoencoder decoder, generative model conditioned on <math id="A2.S4.I1.i2.I0.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{(j)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{(j)}</annotation></semantics></math>, rule-based instantiation). <math id="A2.S4.I1.i2.I0.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{V}</annotation></semantics></math> may be stochastic.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.S4.I1.i2.I0.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="A2.S4.I1.i2.I0.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S4.I1.i2.I0.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Output <math id="A2.S4.I1.i2.I0.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{(i)}=f_{V}(\phi^{(j)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{(i)}=f_{V}(\phi^{(j)})</annotation></semantics></math>, ensuring result resides at level <math id="A2.S4.I1.i2.I0.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.S5" class="ltx_section">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">B.5. </span>Retrieval (<math id="A2.S5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.S5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>)</h4>

<div id="A2.S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These operators manage memory access.</p>
<ul id="A2.S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Query Function (<math id="A2.S5.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>):</span> Generates retrieval cue.</p>
<table id="A2.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q:\Phi_{active}\rightarrow\Phi_{query}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q:\Phi_{active}\rightarrow\Phi_{query}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.S5.I1.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Algorithm sketch:</p>
<ol id="A2.S5.I1.i1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A2.S5.I1.i1.I0.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="A2.S5.I1.i1.I0.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S5.I1.i1.I0.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Input <math id="A2.S5.I1.i1.I0.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{active}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{active}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.S5.I1.i1.I0.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="A2.S5.I1.i1.I0.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S5.I1.i1.I0.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Identify trigger (e.g., active goal, low <math id="A2.S5.I1.i1.I0.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>, associative link).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.S5.I1.i1.I0.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="A2.S5.I1.i1.I0.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S5.I1.i1.I0.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Extract salient elements, unresolved variables, or context related to the trigger from <math id="A2.S5.I1.i1.I0.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{active}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{active}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.S5.I1.i1.I0.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="A2.S5.I1.i1.I0.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S5.I1.i1.I0.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Format these elements into a structured query <math id="A2.S5.I1.i1.I0.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{query}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{query}</annotation></semantics></math> (which may be a single <math id="A2.S5.I1.i1.I0.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> or a minimal state <math id="A2.S5.I1.i1.I0.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.S5.I1.i1.I0.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(5)</span> 
<div id="A2.S5.I1.i1.I0.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S5.I1.i1.I0.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Return <math id="A2.S5.I1.i1.I0.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{query}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{query}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Retrieval Operator (<math id="A2.S5.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>):</span> Accesses memory based on cue, returning a belief state composed of relevant fragments.</p>
<table id="A2.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R:\Phi_{memory}\times\Phi_{query}\rightarrow\Phi" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R:\Phi_{memory}\times\Phi_{query}\rightarrow\Phi</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="A2.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{retrieved}=R(\Phi_{memory},\phi_{query})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{retrieved}=R(\Phi_{memory},\phi_{query})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.S5.I1.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Algorithm sketch:</p>
<ol id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Input <math id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{memory}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{memory}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{query}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{query}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Define relevance threshold <math id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{retrieval}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{retrieval}</annotation></semantics></math> and potentially search scope (e.g., specific <math id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Initialize candidate set <math id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{retrieved}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{retrieved}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each candidate memory fragment <math id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\varphi_{k}\}\in\Phi_{memory}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\varphi_{k}\}\in\Phi_{memory}</annotation></semantics></math> (within scope):</p>
<ul id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i4.I1.i1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Calculate relevance score <math id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(\varphi_{k},\phi_{query})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(\varphi_{k},\phi_{query})</annotation></semantics></math> using semantic distance <math id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, link traversal, pattern matching, etc.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i4.I1.i2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider persistence <math id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{k}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{k}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> or anchoring <math id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i4.I1.i3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i4.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(\varphi_{k},\phi_{query})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(\varphi_{k},\phi_{query})</annotation></semantics></math> exceeds <math id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i4.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{retrieval}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{retrieval}</annotation></semantics></math> (and potentially <math id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i4.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{k}(t)&gt;\delta_{retrieval}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{k}(t)&gt;\delta_{retrieval}</annotation></semantics></math>), add <math id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i4.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> to the candidate set <math id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i4.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{retrieved}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{retrieved}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(5)</span> 
<div id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{retrieved}\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{retrieved}\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<ul id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i5.I1.i1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Select/filter/rank candidates from <math id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i5.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{retrieved}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{retrieved}</annotation></semantics></math> as needed.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i5.I1.i2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Construct the output state <math id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i5.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{retrieved}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{retrieved}</annotation></semantics></math> as a structured ensemble composed of the final selected fragments <math id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i5.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\varphi_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\varphi_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(6)</span> 
<div id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Else (<math id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{retrieved}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{retrieved}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>):</p>
<ul id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i6.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i6.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i6.I1.i1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i6.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i6.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Set <math id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i6.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{retrieved}=\phi_{null}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{retrieved}=\phi_{null}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i6.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{null}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{null}</annotation></semantics></math> is a designated null state in <math id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i6.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(7)</span> 
<div id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Return <math id="A2.S5.I1.i2.I0.i7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{retrieved}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{retrieved}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.S6" class="ltx_section">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">B.6. </span>Meta-Assimilation (<math id="A2.S6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>)</h4>

<div id="A2.S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Integrates introspective information <math id="A2.S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{introspective}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{introspective}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
<table id="A2.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M:\Phi\times\Phi_{introspective}\rightarrow\Phi" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M:\Phi\times\Phi_{introspective}\rightarrow\Phi</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="A2.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{new}=M(\phi_{current},\phi_{introspective})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{new}=M(\phi_{current},\phi_{introspective})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.S6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Algorithm sketch:</p>
<ol id="A2.S6.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A2.S6.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="A2.S6.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S6.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Input <math id="A2.S6.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{current}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{current}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.S6.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{introspective}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{introspective}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.S6.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="A2.S6.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S6.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Identify target sector, typically <math id="A2.S6.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{refl}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{refl}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.S6.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="A2.S6.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S6.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Process <math id="A2.S6.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{introspective}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{introspective}</annotation></semantics></math> to form explicit meta-belief expressions <math id="A2.S6.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{meta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{meta}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., "<math id="A2.S6.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> is low", "Load <math id="A2.S6.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is high"). This might involve formatting, tagging, or confidence assignment based on the nature of the introspective input.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.S6.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="A2.S6.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S6.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Integrate <math id="A2.S6.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{meta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{meta}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="A2.S6.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{current}|_{\Sigma_{refl}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{current}|_{\Sigma_{refl}}</annotation></semantics></math>, potentially using mechanisms similar to <math id="A2.S6.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> (checking for consistency with existing meta-beliefs, handling conflicts within <math id="A2.S6.I1.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{refl}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{refl}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.S6.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(5)</span> 
<div id="A2.S6.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S6.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Update anchoring <math id="A2.S6.I1.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for new or modified meta-beliefs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.S6.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(6)</span> 
<div id="A2.S6.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S6.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Return <math id="A2.S6.I1.i6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{new}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{new}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.S7" class="ltx_section">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">B.7. </span>Spontaneous Drift (<math id="A2.S7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>)</h4>

<div id="A2.S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Models minimal emergence from <math id="A2.S7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
<table id="A2.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D:\Phi\rightarrow\Phi" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D:\Phi\rightarrow\Phi</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.S7.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Conceptual definition: If <math id="A2.S7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\in\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\in\Omega</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.S7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math> produces <math id="A2.S7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> which is a minimal semantic perturbation, potentially <math id="A2.S7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{\prime}\notin\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∉</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{\prime}\notin\Omega</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="A2.S7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\notin\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\notin\Omega</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.S7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math> might represent minimal background noise or diffusion. The specific mechanism is highly dependent on implementation (<math id="A2.S7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>) and likely involves low-magnitude random changes in the state representation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.S8" class="ltx_section">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">B.8. </span>Regulatory Functions (Examples)</h4>

<div id="A2.S8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These are often components used by operators or policies, rather than top-level operators themselves.</p>
<ul id="A2.S8.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.S8.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.S8.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S8.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.S8.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Projection (<math id="A2.S8.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\omega}(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ω</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\omega}(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math>):</span> Component of Semantic Orientation (Chapter <a href="#Ch22" title="Chapter 22 Semantic Orientation ‣ Part VI Regulation and Control: Compass, Gauge, Identity ‣ Theoretical Foundations for Semantic Cognition in Artificial Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a>). Given <math id="A2.S8.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> and axis <math id="A2.S8.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[\omega\rightarrow\omega^{(\infty)}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega\rightarrow\omega^{(\infty)}]</annotation></semantics></math> with vector <math id="A2.S8.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{v}_{\omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>ω</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{v}_{\omega}</annotation></semantics></math>, calculates the projection of <math id="A2.S8.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\phi-\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\phi-\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> onto <math id="A2.S8.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{v}_{\omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>ω</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{v}_{\omega}</annotation></semantics></math>. E.g., <math id="A2.S8.I1.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\omega}(\phi)=\omega+\frac{\langle\phi-\omega,\vec{v}_{\omega}\rangle}{\|\vec{v}_{\omega}\|^{2}}\cdot\vec{v}_{\omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ω</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>ω</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>ω</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>ω</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\omega}(\phi)=\omega+\frac{\langle\phi-\omega,\vec{v}_{\omega}\rangle}{\|\vec{v}_{\omega}\|^{2}}\cdot\vec{v}_{\omega}</annotation></semantics></math> (vector space embedding assumed).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.S8.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.S8.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S8.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.S8.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Angular Deviation (<math id="A2.S8.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta(\phi,\vec{v}_{\omega})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>ω</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta(\phi,\vec{v}_{\omega})</annotation></semantics></math>):</span> Component of Semantic Orientation. Calculates angle between <math id="A2.S8.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\phi-\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\phi-\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.S8.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{v}_{\omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>ω</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{v}_{\omega}</annotation></semantics></math>. E.g., <math id="A2.S8.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta=\cos^{-1}\left(\frac{\langle\phi-\omega,\vec{v}_{\omega}\rangle}{\|\phi-\omega\|\cdot\|\vec{v}_{\omega}\|}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>cos</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>ω</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>ω</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta=\cos^{-1}\left(\frac{\langle\phi-\omega,\vec{v}_{\omega}\rangle}{\|\phi-\omega\|\cdot\|\vec{v}_{\omega}\|}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.S8.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.S8.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.S8.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.S8.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reflective Diagnostic (<math id="A2.S8.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{a}(\phi_{refl})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{a}(\phi_{refl})</annotation></semantics></math>):</span> Component of Semantic Execution gating (Chapter <a href="#Ch25" title="Chapter 25 Semantic Execution ‣ Part VII Embodiment and Action: Executing Belief ‣ Theoretical Foundations for Semantic Cognition in Artificial Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25</span></a>). Takes reflective state <math id="A2.S8.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{refl}=\phi|_{\Sigma_{refl}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{refl}=\phi|_{\Sigma_{refl}}</annotation></semantics></math> and potential action <math id="A2.S8.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> as input. Outputs ’approve’, ’delay’, or ’suppress’ based on evaluating <math id="A2.S8.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> against goals, values, constraints represented in <math id="A2.S8.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{refl}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{refl}</annotation></semantics></math>. Mechanism depends on how these are represented.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A2.S8.p1.2" class="ltx_p">These formal definitions provide a more structured view of the intended behavior of the core cognitive operators within the Semantic Manifold framework.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Detailed Geometric and Topological Considerations</h3>

<section id="A3.S1" class="ltx_section">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">C.1. </span>Introduction</h4>

<div id="A3.S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The main text frequently employs geometric and topological language to describe the semantic state space <math id="A3.S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>, belief dynamics <math id="A3.S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, and regulatory mechanisms. Terms like manifold, distance (<math id="A3.S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>), trajectory, flow (<math id="A3.S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>), boundaries (<math id="A3.S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\mathcal{A}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\mathcal{A}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math>), projection (<math id="A3.S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ω</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\omega}</annotation></semantics></math>), and gauge symmetry (<math id="A3.S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sim_{gauge}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim_{gauge}</annotation></semantics></math>) suggest an underlying mathematical structure. This appendix elaborates on these concepts, providing more formal context and discussing their theoretical implications within the Semantic Manifold framework, while acknowledging that rigorously defining these structures for complex belief spaces remains a significant challenge.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.S2" class="ltx_section">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">C.2. </span>The Semantic Manifold (<math id="A3.S2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>) as a Structured Space</h4>

<div id="A3.S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conceptualize <math id="A3.S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> as a "manifold"—a space that locally resembles a simpler Euclidean space but may have a complex global structure. This is primarily a conceptual tool, as the exact dimensionality and structure are highly dependent on the representation mode <math id="A3.S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> chosen in the agent’s architecture <math id="A3.S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
<ul id="A3.S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A3.S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">High Dimensionality:</span> If <math id="A3.S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> involves embeddings or complex symbolic structures, <math id="A3.S2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> is likely very high-dimensional.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stratification:</span> As discussed in Chapters <a href="#Ch10" title="Chapter 10 Semantic Scaling ‣ Part III Structuring Belief: Modularity and Abstraction ‣ Theoretical Foundations for Semantic Cognition in Artificial Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> and <a href="#Ch11" title="Chapter 11 Semantic Sectors ‣ Part III Structuring Belief: Modularity and Abstraction ‣ Theoretical Foundations for Semantic Cognition in Artificial Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>, <math id="A3.S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> is structured by abstraction levels (<math id="A3.S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi=\bigcup_{k}\Phi^{(k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi=\bigcup_{k}\Phi^{(k)}</annotation></semantics></math>) and functional sectors (<math id="A3.S2.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi=\bigcup_{\Sigma}\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi=\bigcup_{\Sigma}\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>). This suggests <math id="A3.S2.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> might be viewed as a stratified space or perhaps a product space (e.g., locally <math id="A3.S2.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx\mathbb{R}^{N}\times\mathcal{S}\times\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx\mathbb{R}^{N}\times\mathcal{S}\times\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A3.S2.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> represents sectors).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Non-Standard Manifold Properties:</span> Depending on <math id="A3.S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.S2.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> might lack properties of smooth differential manifolds (e.g., if based on discrete graphs or symbolic structures). The geometric intuition remains useful even if formal manifold properties do not strictly hold everywhere.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A3.S2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The key idea is that <math id="A3.S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> possesses sufficient structure to support notions of proximity, paths, and transformations that can be analyzed geometrically.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.S3" class="ltx_section">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">C.3. </span>Metric Structure (<math id="A3.S3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>)</h4>

<div id="A3.S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A crucial element for geometric reasoning is the semantic distance metric <math id="A3.S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(\phi_{1},\phi_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(\phi_{1},\phi_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. As postulated in Chapter <a href="#Ch12" title="Chapter 12 Semantic Geometry ‣ Part III Structuring Belief: Modularity and Abstraction ‣ Theoretical Foundations for Semantic Cognition in Artificial Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>, this function should ideally satisfy the standard metric axioms:</p>
<ol id="A3.S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A3.S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="A3.S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Non-negativity: <math id="A3.S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(\phi_{1},\phi_{2})\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(\phi_{1},\phi_{2})\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="A3.S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Identity of Indiscernibles: <math id="A3.S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(\phi_{1},\phi_{2})=0\iff\phi_{1}=\phi_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">⇔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(\phi_{1},\phi_{2})=0\iff\phi_{1}=\phi_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="A3.S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Symmetry: <math id="A3.S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(\phi_{1},\phi_{2})=d(\phi_{2},\phi_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(\phi_{1},\phi_{2})=d(\phi_{2},\phi_{1})</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.S3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="A3.S3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.S3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Triangle Inequality: <math id="A3.S3.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(\phi_{1},\phi_{3})\leq d(\phi_{1},\phi_{2})+d(\phi_{2},\phi_{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(\phi_{1},\phi_{3})\leq d(\phi_{1},\phi_{2})+d(\phi_{2},\phi_{3})</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="A3.S3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The challenge lies in defining and learning a <math id="A3.S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> that meaningfully reflects semantic similarity over complex belief structures.</p>
<ul id="A3.S3.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A3.S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.S3.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Embedding Spaces (<math id="A3.S3.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>≈</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\approx</annotation></semantics></math> Embedding):</span> <math id="A3.S3.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> could be based on Euclidean distance, cosine distance, or learned Mahalanobis distances in the embedding space. Learning <math id="A3.S3.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> equates to learning the embedding function (Chapter <a href="#Ch34" title="Chapter 34 Learning Semantic Structures ‣ Part IX Learning and Adaptation ‣ Theoretical Foundations for Semantic Cognition in Artificial Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">34</span></a>, Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Implementation Strategies and Examples ‣ Part XI Conclusion ‣ Theoretical Foundations for Semantic Cognition in Artificial Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.S3.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Symbolic Spaces (<math id="A3.S3.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>≈</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\approx</annotation></semantics></math> Symbolic/Graph):</span> <math id="A3.S3.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> might involve weighted edit distances, graph kernel similarities, or distances based on logical entailment or semantic feature overlap. Defining these to satisfy metric axioms and capture semantics well is non-trivial.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A3.S3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">A well-defined metric <math id="A3.S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> grounds notions of proximity, enables similarity-based retrieval (<math id="A3.S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>), allows defining neighborhoods for local processing, and provides a basis for quantifying coherence (<math id="A3.S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>) or trajectory properties (e.g., path length). The difficulty in defining a perfect, universal <math id="A3.S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> underscores why some framework components (like <math id="A3.S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>) might rely on multiple similarity heuristics rather than a single metric.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.3.1. </span>Illustrative Metric Formulations</h5>

<div id="A3.S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The following are simplified, conceptual examples showing how <math id="A3.S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> might be formulated depending on <math id="A3.S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>. These are illustrative, not definitive.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A3.S3.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A3.S3.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.S3.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.S3.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.S3.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Embedding Example (<math id="A3.S3.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> = Embedding):</span> Let <math id="A3.S3.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{embed}(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>embed</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{embed}(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math> be a vector representation (e.g., average embedding of its constituent <math id="A3.S3.I3.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>). Possible metrics include:</p>
<ul id="A3.S3.I3.i1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A3.S3.I3.i1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A3.S3.I3.i1.I1.i1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="A3.S3.I3.i1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.S3.I3.i1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Cosine Distance: <math id="A3.S3.I3.i1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(\phi_{1},\phi_{2})=1-\frac{\text{embed}(\phi_{1})\cdot\text{embed}(\phi_{2})}{\|\text{embed}(\phi_{1})\|\|\text{embed}(\phi_{2})\|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>embed</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext>embed</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mtext>embed</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mtext>embed</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(\phi_{1},\phi_{2})=1-\frac{\text{embed}(\phi_{1})\cdot\text{embed}(\phi_{2})}{\|\text{embed}(\phi_{1})\|\|\text{embed}(\phi_{2})\|}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.S3.I3.i1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A3.S3.I3.i1.I1.i2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="A3.S3.I3.i1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.S3.I3.i1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Euclidean Distance: <math id="A3.S3.I3.i1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(\phi_{1},\phi_{2})=\|\text{embed}(\phi_{1})-\text{embed}(\phi_{2})\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>embed</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mtext>embed</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(\phi_{1},\phi_{2})=\|\text{embed}(\phi_{1})-\text{embed}(\phi_{2})\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A3.S3.I3.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Learning involves optimizing the ‘embed‘ function itself (Chapter <a href="#Ch34" title="Chapter 34 Learning Semantic Structures ‣ Part IX Learning and Adaptation ‣ Theoretical Foundations for Semantic Cognition in Artificial Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">34</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.S3.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.S3.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.S3.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.S3.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Symbolic/Graph Example (<math id="A3.S3.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> = Graph):</span> Let <math id="A3.S3.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{graph}(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>graph</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{graph}(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math> be a graph representation. A conceptual metric might be:</p>
<table id="A3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(\phi_{1},\phi_{2})\approx w_{1}\cdot\text{GraphEditDistance}(\text{graph}(\phi_{1}),\text{graph}(\phi_{2}))+w_{2}\cdot\Delta_{\text{NodeSemantics}}(\phi_{1},\phi_{2})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext>GraphEditDistance</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>graph</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mtext>graph</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mtext>NodeSemantics</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(\phi_{1},\phi_{2})\approx w_{1}\cdot\text{GraphEditDistance}(\text{graph}(\phi_{1}),\text{graph}(\phi_{2}))+w_{2}\cdot\Delta_{\text{NodeSemantics}}(\phi_{1},\phi_{2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.S3.I3.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where the first term measures structural difference and the second measures semantic differences between corresponding nodes, with learned weights <math id="A3.S3.I3.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{1},w_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{1},w_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Defining these components rigorously is complex.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3.S4" class="ltx_section">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">C.4. </span>Topological Considerations</h4>

<div id="A3.S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond metric properties, basic topological concepts are relevant:</p>
<ul id="A3.S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A3.S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Neighborhoods:</span> The metric <math id="A3.S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> induces a topology on <math id="A3.S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>, where open balls <math id="A3.S4.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(\phi,\epsilon)=\{\phi^{\prime}\mid d(\phi,\phi^{\prime})&lt;\epsilon\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(\phi,\epsilon)=\{\phi^{\prime}\mid d(\phi,\phi^{\prime})&lt;\epsilon\}</annotation></semantics></math> define neighborhoods. This formalizes the notion of "nearby" belief states.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Continuity:</span> Ideally, cognitive operators (<math id="A3.S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A,N_{t},\Lambda,V,M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A,N_{t},\Lambda,V,M</annotation></semantics></math>) should exhibit some form of continuity: small changes in input <math id="A3.S4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> lead to small changes in output <math id="A3.S4.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math>, except at specific points of discontinuity (like those potentially involved in <math id="A3.S4.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{corr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{corr}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A3.S4.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>). This ensures smooth belief evolution in most cases. Proving continuity depends heavily on the specific implementation of <math id="A3.S4.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> and the operators.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Connectedness:</span> Are activation basins <math id="A3.S4.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> connected regions? Can any two points within <math id="A3.S4.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> be joined by a path entirely within <math id="A3.S4.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math>? Connectedness impacts how easily a trajectory can enter or leave a basin. Fragmentation of basins could represent complex disjunctive conditions for action readiness.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.S4.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.S4.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.S4.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.S4.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Convergence (<math id="A3.S4.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{(\infty)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{(\infty)}</annotation></semantics></math>):</span> The definition of the semantic singularity <math id="A3.S4.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{(\infty)}=\lim_{k\rightarrow\infty}\Lambda^{k}(\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{(\infty)}=\lim_{k\rightarrow\infty}\Lambda^{k}(\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> requires the sequence of abstractions to converge in the topology induced by <math id="A3.S4.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. This implies that repeated abstraction eventually leads to diminishing changes, settling towards a fixed point. Conditions for convergence might involve the abstraction operators <math id="A3.S4.I1.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> being contractive mappings under <math id="A3.S4.I1.i4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A3.S4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">These topological notions help formalize intuitions about smooth evolution, regions of readiness, and the limiting behavior of core processes like abstraction.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.S5" class="ltx_section">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">C.5. </span>Semantic Flow Fields (<math id="A3.S5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>)</h4>

<div id="A3.S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Chapter <a href="#Ch12" title="Chapter 12 Semantic Geometry ‣ Part III Structuring Belief: Modularity and Abstraction ‣ Theoretical Foundations for Semantic Cognition in Artificial Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> introduced the idea of modeling belief evolution <math id="A3.S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(t)</annotation></semantics></math> via a semantic flow field <math id="A3.S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F:\Phi\rightarrow T\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F:\Phi\rightarrow T\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> (where <math id="A3.S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> is the tangent space, representing possible directions of change), such that <math id="A3.S5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{d\gamma}{dt}=F(\gamma(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{d\gamma}{dt}=F(\gamma(t))</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
<ul id="A3.S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A3.S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Vector Field Concept:</span> <math id="A3.S5.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math> specifies the instantaneous velocity (direction and magnitude) of belief change at state <math id="A3.S5.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>. This field encapsulates the combined effects of internal dynamics (<math id="A3.S5.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{t},D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{t},D</annotation></semantics></math>, background <math id="A3.S5.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A,M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A,M</annotation></semantics></math>) and potentially control signals (<math id="A3.S5.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{regulate}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{regulate}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Challenges:</span> Defining and computing <math id="A3.S5.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> globally over high-dimensional <math id="A3.S5.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> is extremely challenging. <math id="A3.S5.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is likely non-stationary (changing based on context, goals, external input <math id="A3.S5.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X(s)</annotation></semantics></math>) and highly complex. In practice, <math id="A3.S5.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> serves more as a conceptual tool for understanding local dynamics and attractor landscapes.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.S5.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fixed Points:</span> States <math id="A3.S5.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A3.S5.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\phi^{*})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\phi^{*})=0</annotation></semantics></math> are fixed points. Stable fixed points act as attractors (representing coherent schemas, memories, goals), while unstable ones act as repellers (e.g., points of high contradiction). The landscape of these fixed points shapes the overall dynamics.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.S5.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.S5.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.S5.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.S5.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Relation to Operators:</span> Specific operators correspond to specific components or modifications of <math id="A3.S5.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>. Assimilation <math id="A3.S5.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> injects change, <math id="A3.S5.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> provides a drift towards <math id="A3.S5.I1.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math>, orientation control adjusts <math id="A3.S5.I1.i4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> to align with axes <math id="A3.S5.I1.i4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{v}_{\omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>ω</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{v}_{\omega}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A3.S5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Viewing dynamics through flow fields provides intuition about stability, convergence, and the forces shaping belief trajectories, even if <math id="A3.S5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> itself is rarely explicitly computed.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.5.1. </span>Illustrative Coherence Formulation (<math id="A3.S5.SS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>)</h5>

<div id="A3.S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Coherence (<math id="A3.S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>, Chapter <a href="#Ch31" title="Chapter 31 Cognitive Load ‣ Part VIII Meta-Cognition and Recursive Regulation ‣ Theoretical Foundations for Semantic Cognition in Artificial Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">31</span></a>) measures internal consistency. Example formulations depend heavily on <math id="A3.S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A3.S5.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A3.S5.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.S5.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.S5.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.S5.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Symbolic Example (<math id="A3.S5.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> = Logic Fragments):</span> Let <math id="A3.S5.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Contradict}(\varphi_{i},\varphi_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Contradict</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Contradict}(\varphi_{i},\varphi_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> be a predicate checking for logical contradiction. A simple measure could be:</p>
<table id="A3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa(\phi)=1-\frac{|\{(\varphi_{i},\varphi_{j})\in\phi\times\phi\mid\text{Contradict}(\varphi_{i},\varphi_{j})\}|}{|\phi|^{2}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mtext>Contradict</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa(\phi)=1-\frac{|\{(\varphi_{i},\varphi_{j})\in\phi\times\phi\mid\text{Contradict}(\varphi_{i},\varphi_{j})\}|}{|\phi|^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.S5.I2.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">(Normalized count of contradictions, where 1 is perfect coherence). More sophisticated measures might weight contradictions based on belief strength or relevance.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.S5.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.S5.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.S5.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.S5.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Embedding Example (<math id="A3.S5.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> = Embedding):</span> Coherence might relate to the compactness or variance of the embeddings of belief fragments <math id="A3.S5.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{i}\in\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{i}\in\phi</annotation></semantics></math>. Low variance might indicate high coherence.</p>
<table id="A3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa(\phi)\approx\frac{1}{1+\text{Variance}(\{\text{embed}(\varphi_{i})\mid\varphi_{i}\in\phi\})}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mtext>Variance</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mtext>embed</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa(\phi)\approx\frac{1}{1+\text{Variance}(\{\text{embed}(\varphi_{i})\mid\varphi_{i}\in\phi\})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.S5.I2.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Alternatively, it could be the output of a learned classifier trained to predict consistency, or derived from the potential function of an energy-based model trained on coherent belief states.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A3.S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These illustrate how <math id="A3.S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> aims to capture internal consistency, but its practical calculation varies greatly.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.5.2. </span>Illustrative Cognitive Load Formulation (<math id="A3.S5.SS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A3.S5.SS2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>)</h5>

<div id="A3.S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Cognitive Load (<math id="A3.S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, Chapter <a href="#Ch31" title="Chapter 31 Cognitive Load ‣ Part VIII Meta-Cognition and Recursive Regulation ‣ Theoretical Foundations for Semantic Cognition in Artificial Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">31</span></a>), often proxied by <math id="A3.S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, represents the demand on processing resources. A conceptual formula might combine static and dynamic factors:</p>
<table id="A3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(\phi(t))\approx c_{1}\cdot\text{Complexity}(\phi(t))+c_{2}\cdot\sum_{\Sigma}\alpha_{\Sigma}(t)\cdot\text{Cost}(\Sigma)+c_{3}\cdot\text{Rate}(\text{Ops},\Delta t)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext>Complexity</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.055em">⋅</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></munder><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mtext>Cost</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext>Rate</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>Ops</mtext><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(\phi(t))\approx c_{1}\cdot\text{Complexity}(\phi(t))+c_{2}\cdot\sum_{\Sigma}\alpha_{\Sigma}(t)\cdot\text{Cost}(\Sigma)+c_{3}\cdot\text{Rate}(\text{Ops},\Delta t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.S5.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where:</p>
<ul id="A3.S5.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A3.S5.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.S5.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.S5.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A3.S5.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Complexity}(\phi(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Complexity</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Complexity}(\phi(t))</annotation></semantics></math>: Measures static complexity (e.g., number of active <math id="A3.S5.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, structural complexity like graph density).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.S5.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.S5.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.S5.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A3.S5.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{\Sigma}\alpha_{\Sigma}(t)\cdot\text{Cost}(\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext>Cost</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{\Sigma}\alpha_{\Sigma}(t)\cdot\text{Cost}(\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math>: Weighted sum of active sectors, where <math id="A3.S5.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Cost}(\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Cost</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Cost}(\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> reflects the inherent processing demand of sector <math id="A3.S5.I3.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.S5.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.S5.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.S5.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A3.S5.I3.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Rate}(\text{Ops},\Delta t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Rate</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>Ops</mtext><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Rate}(\text{Ops},\Delta t)</annotation></semantics></math>: Measures recent dynamic intensity (e.g., number/complexity of <math id="A3.S5.I3.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A,M,R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A,M,R</annotation></semantics></math> operations in the last time window <math id="A3.S5.I3.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta t</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A3.S5.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The coefficients <math id="A3.S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1},c_{2},c_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1},c_{2},c_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> are weights. This formula is purely illustrative; actual measures would need calibration based on the specific architecture <math id="A3.S5.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> and implementation.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3.S6" class="ltx_section">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">C.6. </span>Gauge Symmetry (<math id="A3.S6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sim_{\text{gauge}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>gauge</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim_{\text{gauge}}</annotation></semantics></math>)</h4>

<div id="A3.S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Semantic gauge equivalence <math id="A3.S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\phi_{1}\sim_{gauge}\phi_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mi>ϕ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{1}\sim_{gauge}\phi_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> (Chapter <a href="#Ch23" title="Chapter 23 Semantic Gauge ‣ Part VI Regulation and Control: Compass, Gauge, Identity ‣ Theoretical Foundations for Semantic Cognition in Artificial Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>) relates states that are functionally indistinguishable. Mathematically:</p>
<ul id="A3.S6.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A3.S6.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.S6.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.S6.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.S6.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Equivalence Relation:</span> <math id="A3.S6.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sim_{gauge}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim_{gauge}</annotation></semantics></math> partitions <math id="A3.S6.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> into disjoint equivalence classes <math id="A3.S6.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\phi]_{gauge}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\phi]_{gauge}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.S6.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.S6.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.S6.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.S6.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Quotient Space:</span> The space of functionally distinct states can be thought of as the quotient space <math id="A3.S6.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Phi/\sim_{gauge}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo rspace="0em">/</mo><mo lspace="0em">∼</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi/\sim_{gauge}</annotation></semantics></math>, where each point represents an entire gauge class. Cognitive dynamics that respect gauge symmetry effectively operate on this quotient space.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.S6.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.S6.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.S6.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.S6.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fiber Bundle Analogy (Speculative):</span> In physics, gauge theories often involve fiber bundles, where the base space represents the physically relevant degrees of freedom and the fibers represent gauge-equivalent internal configurations. Analogously, <math id="A3.S6.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> could potentially be viewed as a bundle where the base space is <math id="A3.S6.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Phi/\sim_{gauge}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo rspace="0em">/</mo><mo lspace="0em">∼</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi/\sim_{gauge}</annotation></semantics></math> (functional states) and the fibers are the gauge classes <math id="A3.S6.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\phi]_{gauge}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\phi]_{gauge}</annotation></semantics></math> (representational variations). Operators respecting gauge symmetry act primarily on the base space, while gauge transformations move within fibers. This is a highly speculative analogy requiring rigorous development.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A3.S6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The mathematical perspective highlights gauge symmetry as a fundamental structural property reducing the effective complexity of the state space by identifying functionally redundant representations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.S7" class="ltx_section">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">C.7. </span>Conclusion</h4>

<div id="A3.S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Applying concepts from geometry and topology provides powerful tools for analyzing the structure and dynamics of the semantic state space <math id="A3.S7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>. Viewing <math id="A3.S7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> as a structured manifold, potentially equipped with a metric <math id="A3.S7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and supporting flow fields <math id="A3.S7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>, allows us to model belief evolution <math id="A3.S7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(t)</annotation></semantics></math> in intuitive ways. Concepts like neighborhoods, continuity, connectedness, convergence, fixed points, and partitions under gauge symmetry (<math id="A3.S7.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sim_{gauge}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim_{gauge}</annotation></semantics></math>) offer a richer language for describing cognitive processes. The illustrative formulations provided for <math id="A3.S7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.S7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A3.S7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> demonstrate how these abstract concepts might be concretized depending on representation <math id="A3.S7.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>, although their precise, robust definition remains a key challenge. While rigorously formalizing these structures for complex belief representations is ongoing work, the geometric perspective itself provides valuable insights into the principles governing structured belief, regulation, and intelligent navigation within the internal landscape of meaning.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Implementation Strategies and Examples</h3>

<section id="A4.S1" class="ltx_section">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">D.1. </span>Introduction</h4>

<div id="A4.S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Semantic Manifold framework provides a high-level functional architecture for structured belief, regulation, and dynamics. Realizing this architecture in a practical computational system requires bridging the abstract definitions of <math id="A4.S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>, its operators (<math id="A4.S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A,M,N_{t},K,\Lambda,V,R,Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A,M,N_{t},K,\Lambda,V,R,Q</annotation></semantics></math>), structures (<math id="A4.S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma,k</annotation></semantics></math>), and metrics (<math id="A4.S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d,\kappa,\lambda,\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d,\kappa,\lambda,\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>) with concrete implementation paradigms.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This appendix explores potential strategies for implementing agents based on the Semantic Manifold principles, explicitly linking these strategies to the representation mode parameter <math id="A4.S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> defined within the architectural configuration <math id="A4.S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> (Chapter <a href="#Ch8" title="Chapter 8 Parameterized Semantic Architectures ‣ Part II Semantic Foundations: The Semantic Substrate ‣ Theoretical Foundations for Semantic Cognition in Artificial Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>). We then present a simplified, illustrative example simulation run to demonstrate how these components might interact dynamically.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.S2" class="ltx_section">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">D.2. </span>Implementation Paradigms (Relating to <math id="A4.S2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>)</h4>

<div id="A4.S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The choice of representation mode <math id="A4.S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> fundamentally shapes how the semantic manifold <math id="A4.S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> and its associated operators are implemented.</p>
</div>
<section id="A4.S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.2.1. </span>Symbolic Implementations (<math id="A4.S2.SS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>≈</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\approx</annotation></semantics></math> Symbolic)</h5>

<div id="A4.S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A4.S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A4.S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Representation:</span> Belief states <math id="A4.S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> are represented using symbolic structures like knowledge graphs (e.g., RDF, property graphs), logical propositions (e.g., first-order logic, description logics), structured databases, or frame systems. Linguistic expressions <math id="A4.S2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are nodes or structured entries.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Operators:</span></p>
<ul id="A4.S2.I1.i2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A4.S2.I1.i2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A4.S2.I1.i2.I1.i1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="A4.S2.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S2.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A4.S2.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A,M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A,M</annotation></semantics></math>: Implemented via graph update algorithms, rule-based inference engines (adding/retracting facts/rules), constraint satisfaction solvers (for <math id="A4.S2.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{corr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{corr}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.S2.I1.i2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A4.S2.I1.i2.I1.i2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="A4.S2.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S2.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A4.S2.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda,V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda,V</annotation></semantics></math>: Implemented using symbolic generalization techniques (e.g., inductive logic programming, conceptual clustering) or predefined hierarchical taxonomies/ontologies.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.S2.I1.i2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A4.S2.I1.i2.I1.i3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="A4.S2.I1.i2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S2.I1.i2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A4.S2.I1.i2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>: Implemented by rules that decrease activation weights or remove nodes/edges based on time-stamps or usage counters (reflecting <math id="A4.S2.I1.i2.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.S2.I1.i2.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A4.S2.I1.i2.I1.i4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="A4.S2.I1.i2.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S2.I1.i2.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A4.S2.I1.i2.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K,K_{\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K,K_{\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>: Implemented by direct node/edge deletion operations.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.S2.I1.i2.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A4.S2.I1.i2.I1.i5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="A4.S2.I1.i2.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S2.I1.i2.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A4.S2.I1.i2.I1.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q,R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q,R</annotation></semantics></math>: Implemented using graph traversal algorithms, database queries, or logical inference to find matching patterns or related concepts based on <math id="A4.S2.I1.i2.I1.i5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{query}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{query}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pros/Cons:</span> Strong potential for interpretability, formal verification, precise logical reasoning. May struggle with ambiguity, scalability, grounding, and learning from unstructured data.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.2.2. </span>Connectionist/Embedding Implementations (<math id="A4.S2.SS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>≈</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\approx</annotation></semantics></math> Embedding)</h5>

<div id="A4.S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A4.S2.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A4.S2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.S2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.S2.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Representation:</span> Belief states <math id="A4.S2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> or fragments <math id="A4.S2.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are represented as vectors in a high-dimensional embedding space (learned via e.g., language models, graph embedding techniques). Structure might be implicit in the vector space geometry or explicitly represented via attention mechanisms or pointer networks.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.S2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.S2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.S2.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Operators:</span> Often implemented as specialized neural network modules:</p>
<ul id="A4.S2.I2.i2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A4.S2.I2.i2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A4.S2.I2.i2.I1.i1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="A4.S2.I2.i2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S2.I2.i2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A4.S2.I2.i2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda,V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda,V</annotation></semantics></math>: Could be encoder/decoder components of autoencoders or sequence-to-sequence models.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.S2.I2.i2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A4.S2.I2.i2.I1.i2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="A4.S2.I2.i2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S2.I2.i2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A4.S2.I2.i2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A,M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A,M</annotation></semantics></math>: Might involve attention mechanisms to combine embeddings of <math id="A4.S2.I2.i2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{current}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{current}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.S2.I2.i2.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{input}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{input}</annotation></semantics></math>, potentially using gated recurrent units or transformers to update the state representation. <math id="A4.S2.I2.i2.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{corr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{corr}</annotation></semantics></math> might involve learning to identify and down-weight conflicting vector components.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.S2.I2.i2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A4.S2.I2.i2.I1.i3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="A4.S2.I2.i2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S2.I2.i2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A4.S2.I2.i2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>: Could be modeled by multiplicative decay applied to activation vectors, potentially modulated by learned gating functions representing anchoring <math id="A4.S2.I2.i2.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.S2.I2.i2.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A4.S2.I2.i2.I1.i4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="A4.S2.I2.i2.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S2.I2.i2.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A4.S2.I2.i2.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>: Might involve setting state vectors to zero or a null embedding. <math id="A4.S2.I2.i2.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> requires mechanisms to zero out specific subspace components representing sectors.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.S2.I2.i2.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A4.S2.I2.i2.I1.i5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="A4.S2.I2.i2.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S2.I2.i2.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A4.S2.I2.i2.I1.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q,R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q,R</annotation></semantics></math>: Implemented using similarity search (e.g., nearest neighbor search based on learned metric <math id="A4.S2.I2.i2.I1.i5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>) in the embedding space based on the query vector derived from <math id="A4.S2.I2.i2.I1.i5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{active}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{active}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.S2.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.S2.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S2.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.S2.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pros/Cons:</span> Naturally handles ambiguity, similarity, and learning from large datasets. Can struggle with interpretability, precise logical inference, and catastrophic forgetting. Structure is often less explicit.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.2.3. </span>Hybrid Implementations (<math id="A4.S2.SS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>≈</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\approx</annotation></semantics></math> Hybrid)</h5>

<div id="A4.S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A4.S2.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A4.S2.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.S2.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S2.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.S2.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Representation:</span> Combines symbolic structures (e.g., a knowledge graph for core facts and relations) with learned embeddings (e.g., for representing nuanced meanings or handling unstructured input). This is often the focus of neuro-symbolic AI.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.S2.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.S2.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S2.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.S2.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Operators:</span> Use a mix of techniques. Symbolic rules might handle high-level reasoning or coherence checks (<math id="A4.S2.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{corr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{corr}</annotation></semantics></math>), while neural modules handle perception (<math id="A4.S2.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>), elaboration (<math id="A4.S2.I3.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{elab},V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{elab},V</annotation></semantics></math>), or similarity-based retrieval (<math id="A4.S2.I3.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>). Operators need well-defined interfaces to interact across representational modes.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.S2.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.S2.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S2.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.S2.I3.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pros/Cons:</span> Aims to combine the strengths of both paradigms—interpretability and precision with learning and generalization. Integration complexity is a major challenge.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.2.4. </span>Role of Large Language Models (LLMs)</h5>

<div id="A4.S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A4.S2.I4" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A4.S2.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.S2.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S2.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.S2.I4.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLMs as Components, Not the Architecture:</span> LLMs can serve as powerful components within a Semantic Manifold architecture (<math id="A4.S2.I4.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{[\theta]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{[\theta]}</annotation></semantics></math>), particularly for implementing specific operators, rather than being the entire architecture itself. The Semantic Manifold framework defines the overall structure, dynamics, and regulation (<math id="A4.S2.I4.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>), which an LLM alone typically lacks (e.g., explicit persistent state <math id="A4.S2.I4.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>, sectors <math id="A4.S2.I4.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, operators <math id="A4.S2.I4.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{t},K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{t},K</annotation></semantics></math>, regulation <math id="A4.S2.I4.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa,\lambda,\epsilon,\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa,\lambda,\epsilon,\theta</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.S2.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.S2.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S2.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.S2.I4.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Potential Implementations using LLMs:</span></p>
<ul id="A4.S2.I4.i2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A4.S2.I4.i2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A4.S2.I4.i2.I1.i1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="A4.S2.I4.i2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S2.I4.i2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A4.S2.I4.i2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>: Using an LLM to parse unstructured input <math id="A4.S2.I4.i2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> into initial belief fragments <math id="A4.S2.I4.i2.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.S2.I4.i2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A4.S2.I4.i2.I1.i2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="A4.S2.I4.i2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S2.I4.i2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A4.S2.I4.i2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{elab},V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{elab},V</annotation></semantics></math>: Using prompted generation to elaborate on input/retrieved memories or instantiate abstract concepts.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.S2.I4.i2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A4.S2.I4.i2.I1.i3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="A4.S2.I4.i2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S2.I4.i2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A4.S2.I4.i2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>: Prompting an LLM with state information (<math id="A4.S2.I4.i2.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa,\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa,\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>) and <math id="A4.S2.I4.i2.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{introspective}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{introspective}</annotation></semantics></math> to generate reflective meta-beliefs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.S2.I4.i2.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A4.S2.I4.i2.I1.i4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="A4.S2.I4.i2.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S2.I4.i2.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A4.S2.I4.i2.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>: Prompting an LLM based on <math id="A4.S2.I4.i2.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{active}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{active}</annotation></semantics></math> to formulate appropriate retrieval queries <math id="A4.S2.I4.i2.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{query}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{query}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.S2.I4.i2.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A4.S2.I4.i2.I1.i5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="A4.S2.I4.i2.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S2.I4.i2.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A4.S2.I4.i2.I1.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>: Using retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), where the LLM’s generation is conditioned on documents retrieved based on <math id="A4.S2.I4.i2.I1.i5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{query}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{query}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.S2.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.S2.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S2.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.S2.I4.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Challenges:</span> Ensuring the LLM component respects the constraints and dynamics of the broader architecture (e.g., coherence <math id="A4.S2.I4.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>, gauge <math id="A4.S2.I4.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sim_{gauge}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim_{gauge}</annotation></semantics></math>), managing state consistency, controlling hallucination, and integrating LLM outputs with other symbolic or numeric components remain key challenges.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4.S3" class="ltx_section">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">D.3. </span>Illustrative Simulation Example</h4>

<div id="A4.S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This highly simplified example illustrates the interplay of operators over a few time steps. Assume a simple symbolic representation (<math id="A4.S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>≈</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\approx</annotation></semantics></math> Symbolic) and basic sectors <math id="A4.S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{perc},\Sigma_{task},\Sigma_{mem}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{perc},\Sigma_{task},\Sigma_{mem}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let the persistence threshold <math id="A4.S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>. Anchoring <math id="A4.S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> defaults to 1 unless specified.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="A4.S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A4.S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="A4.S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Time <math id="A4.S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math>:</span> Initial state near vacuum. <math id="A4.S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{0}\approx\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{0}\approx\Omega</annotation></semantics></math>. Agent receives input <math id="A4.S3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>= "Task: Check Sensor Alpha status."</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="A4.S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Observation Encoding (<math id="A4.S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>):</span> <math id="A4.S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X(s)</annotation></semantics></math> generates <math id="A4.S3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext='\phi_{input}=\{\varphi_{1}:\text{"Goal: Check Sensor Alpha status"}\}' display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mtext>"Goal: Check Sensor Alpha status"</mtext><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{input}=\{\varphi_{1}:\text{"Goal: Check Sensor Alpha status"}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Assign high anchor <math id="A4.S3.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1}=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1}=10</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="A4.S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assimilation (<math id="A4.S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>):</span> <math id="A4.S3.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{1}=A(\phi_{0},\phi_{input})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{1}=A(\phi_{0},\phi_{input})</annotation></semantics></math>. State becomes <math id="A4.S3.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{1}=\{\varphi_{1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{1}=\{\varphi_{1}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume <math id="A4.S3.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> routes this to <math id="A4.S3.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{task}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{task}</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="A4.S3.I1.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}(0)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}(0)=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.S3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="A4.S3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.S3.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Query Generation (<math id="A4.S3.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>):</span> Goal <math id="A4.S3.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> triggers query. <math id="A4.S3.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(\phi_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(\phi_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> generates</p>
<table id="A4.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext='\phi_{query}=\{\text{"Query: Current status of Sensor Alpha?"}\}' display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mtext>"Query: Current status of Sensor Alpha?"</mtext><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{query}=\{\text{"Query: Current status of Sensor Alpha?"}\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.S3.I1.i4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.S3.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(5)</span> 
<div id="A4.S3.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S3.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.S3.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Retrieval (<math id="A4.S3.I1.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>):</span> <math id="A4.S3.I1.i5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> searches <math id="A4.S3.I1.i5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{memory}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{memory}</annotation></semantics></math> using <math id="A4.S3.I1.i5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{query}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{query}</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume it finds a relevant, slightly decayed memory: <math id="A4.S3.I1.i5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext='\phi_{retrieved}=\{\varphi_{2}:\text{"Sensor Alpha reported OK at T-5min"}\}' display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mtext>"Sensor Alpha reported OK at T-5min"</mtext><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{retrieved}=\{\varphi_{2}:\text{"Sensor Alpha reported OK at T-5min"}\}</annotation></semantics></math> with current persistence <math id="A4.S3.I1.i5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{2}(t_{R})=0.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{2}(t_{R})=0.6</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.S3.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(6)</span> 
<div id="A4.S3.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S3.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.S3.I1.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Integration (<math id="A4.S3.I1.i6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>):</span> <math id="A4.S3.I1.i6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{2}=A(\phi_{1},\phi_{retrieved})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{2}=A(\phi_{1},\phi_{retrieved})</annotation></semantics></math>. Operator <math id="A4.S3.I1.i6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> integrates <math id="A4.S3.I1.i6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> (likely into <math id="A4.S3.I1.i6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{mem}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{mem}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A4.S3.I1.i6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{perc}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{perc}</annotation></semantics></math>) and potentially adds elaboration via <math id="A4.S3.I1.i6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{elab}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{elab}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let</p>
<table id="A4.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext='\phi_{2}=\{\varphi_{1},\varphi_{2},\varphi_{3}:\text{"Sensor Alpha status likely OK (based on recent memory)"}\}' display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mtext>"Sensor Alpha status likely OK (based on recent memory)"</mtext><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{2}=\{\varphi_{1},\varphi_{2},\varphi_{3}:\text{"Sensor Alpha status likely OK (based on recent memory)"}\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.S3.I1.i6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">. Operator <math id="A4.S3.I1.i6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> also re-anchors <math id="A4.S3.I1.i6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, increasing <math id="A4.S3.I1.i6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., <math id="A4.S3.I1.i6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{2}=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{2}=5</annotation></semantics></math>) and resetting its persistence <math id="A4.S3.I1.i6.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{2}(0)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{2}(0)=1</annotation></semantics></math>. State persists: <math id="A4.S3.I1.i6.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{2}=\{\varphi_{1}(a=10),\varphi_{2}(a=5),\varphi_{3}(a=1)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{2}=\{\varphi_{1}(a=10),\varphi_{2}(a=5),\varphi_{3}(a=1)\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.S3.I1.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(7)</span> 
<div id="A4.S3.I1.i7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S3.I1.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.S3.I1.i7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Time <math id="A4.S3.I1.i7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=20</annotation></semantics></math> (Nullification <math id="A4.S3.I1.i7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>):</span> Assume no further input related to these beliefs for 20 time units. Let decay <math id="A4.S3.I1.i7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}\approx 0.02/(1+a_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>0.02</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}\approx 0.02/(1+a_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
<ul id="A4.S3.I1.i7.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A4.S3.I1.i7.I0.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.S3.I1.i7.I0.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S3.I1.i7.I0.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A4.S3.I1.i7.I0.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="A4.S3.I1.i7.I0.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}\approx 0.02/11\approx 0.0018" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>0.02</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.0018</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}\approx 0.02/11\approx 0.0018</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="A4.S3.I1.i7.I0.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="d_{1}(20)\approx e^{-0.0018\times 20}\approx 0.96&gt;\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>20</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≈</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.0018</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.96</mn><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}(20)\approx e^{-0.0018\times 20}\approx 0.96&gt;\delta</annotation></semantics></math>. Belief persists.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.S3.I1.i7.I0.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.S3.I1.i7.I0.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S3.I1.i7.I0.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A4.S3.I1.i7.I0.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="A4.S3.I1.i7.I0.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{2}\approx 0.02/6\approx 0.0033" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>0.02</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.0033</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{2}\approx 0.02/6\approx 0.0033</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="A4.S3.I1.i7.I0.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="d_{2}(20)\approx e^{-0.0033\times 20}\approx 0.94&gt;\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>20</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≈</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.0033</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.94</mn><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{2}(20)\approx e^{-0.0033\times 20}\approx 0.94&gt;\delta</annotation></semantics></math>. Belief persists.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.S3.I1.i7.I0.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.S3.I1.i7.I0.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S3.I1.i7.I0.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A4.S3.I1.i7.I0.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="A4.S3.I1.i7.I0.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{3}\approx 0.02/2=0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>0.02</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{3}\approx 0.02/2=0.01</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="A4.S3.I1.i7.I0.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="d_{3}(20)\approx e^{-0.01\times 20}\approx 0.82&gt;\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>20</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≈</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.01</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.82</mn><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{3}(20)\approx e^{-0.01\times 20}\approx 0.82&gt;\delta</annotation></semantics></math>. Belief persists.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A4.S3.I1.i7.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The state remains <math id="A4.S3.I1.i7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{3}=\phi_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{3}=\phi_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, but internal persistence values have decreased.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.S3.I1.i8" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(8)</span> 
<div id="A4.S3.I1.i8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S3.I1.i8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.S3.I1.i8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Time <math id="A4.S3.I1.i8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=100</annotation></semantics></math> (Nullification <math id="A4.S3.I1.i8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>):</span> Assume still no related input.</p>
<ul id="A4.S3.I1.i8.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A4.S3.I1.i8.I0.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.S3.I1.i8.I0.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S3.I1.i8.I0.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A4.S3.I1.i8.I0.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="A4.S3.I1.i8.I0.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="d_{1}(100)\approx e^{-0.0018\times 100}\approx 0.84&gt;\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>100</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≈</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.0018</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.84</mn><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}(100)\approx e^{-0.0018\times 100}\approx 0.84&gt;\delta</annotation></semantics></math>. Persists.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.S3.I1.i8.I0.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.S3.I1.i8.I0.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S3.I1.i8.I0.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A4.S3.I1.i8.I0.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="A4.S3.I1.i8.I0.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="d_{2}(100)\approx e^{-0.0033\times 100}\approx 0.72&gt;\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>100</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≈</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.0033</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.72</mn><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{2}(100)\approx e^{-0.0033\times 100}\approx 0.72&gt;\delta</annotation></semantics></math>. Persists.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.S3.I1.i8.I0.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.S3.I1.i8.I0.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S3.I1.i8.I0.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A4.S3.I1.i8.I0.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="A4.S3.I1.i8.I0.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="d_{3}(100)\approx e^{-0.01\times 100}\approx 0.37&gt;\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>100</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≈</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.01</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.37</mn><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{3}(100)\approx e^{-0.01\times 100}\approx 0.37&gt;\delta</annotation></semantics></math>. Persists.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A4.S3.I1.i8.p1.2" class="ltx_p">State is still <math id="A4.S3.I1.i8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{4}=\phi_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{4}=\phi_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.S3.I1.i9" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(9)</span> 
<div id="A4.S3.I1.i9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S3.I1.i9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.S3.I1.i9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Time <math id="A4.S3.I1.i9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=250" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>250</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=250</annotation></semantics></math> (Nullification <math id="A4.S3.I1.i9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>):</span></p>
<ul id="A4.S3.I1.i9.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A4.S3.I1.i9.I0.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.S3.I1.i9.I0.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S3.I1.i9.I0.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A4.S3.I1.i9.I0.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="A4.S3.I1.i9.I0.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="d_{1}(250)\approx e^{-0.0018\times 250}\approx 0.64&gt;\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>250</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≈</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.0018</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>250</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.64</mn><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}(250)\approx e^{-0.0018\times 250}\approx 0.64&gt;\delta</annotation></semantics></math>. Persists.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.S3.I1.i9.I0.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.S3.I1.i9.I0.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S3.I1.i9.I0.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A4.S3.I1.i9.I0.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="A4.S3.I1.i9.I0.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="d_{2}(250)\approx e^{-0.0033\times 250}\approx 0.44&gt;\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>250</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≈</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.0033</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>250</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.44</mn><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{2}(250)\approx e^{-0.0033\times 250}\approx 0.44&gt;\delta</annotation></semantics></math>. Persists.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.S3.I1.i9.I0.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.S3.I1.i9.I0.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S3.I1.i9.I0.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A4.S3.I1.i9.I0.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="A4.S3.I1.i9.I0.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="d_{3}(250)\approx e^{-0.01\times 250}\approx 0.08&lt;\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>250</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≈</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.01</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>250</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.08</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{3}(250)\approx e^{-0.01\times 250}\approx 0.08&lt;\delta</annotation></semantics></math>. Decays!</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A4.S3.I1.i9.p1.2" class="ltx_p">State becomes <math id="A4.S3.I1.i9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{5}=\{\varphi_{1},\varphi_{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{5}=\{\varphi_{1},\varphi_{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The weakly anchored elaboration <math id="A4.S3.I1.i9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> has faded due to <math id="A4.S3.I1.i9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. The goal <math id="A4.S3.I1.i9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and the re-anchored memory <math id="A4.S3.I1.i9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> remain.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="A4.S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This simplified trace illustrates how input (<math id="A4.S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>), integration (<math id="A4.S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>), memory access (<math id="A4.S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q,R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q,R</annotation></semantics></math>), and decay (<math id="A4.S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>) interact, mediated by anchoring (<math id="A4.S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>), to shape the belief state over time.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.S4" class="ltx_section">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">D.4. </span>Challenges and Considerations</h4>

<div id="A4.S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Implementing the Semantic Manifold framework involves several practical challenges:</p>
<ul id="A4.S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A4.S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scalability:</span> Managing potentially vast belief spaces <math id="A4.S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> and complex operator interactions efficiently requires careful architectural design and optimization, regardless of <math id="A4.S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Integration Across Paradigms (Hybrid <math id="A4.S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>):</span> Ensuring seamless interaction and semantic consistency between symbolic and embedding-based components is non-trivial.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Operator Definition Detail:</span> The abstract definitions must be translated into concrete, robust algorithms with well-defined behavior for edge cases and complex inputs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.S4.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.S4.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S4.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.S4.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Parameter Tuning (<math id="A4.S4.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>):</span> Even if not learning <math id="A4.S4.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> itself (Chapter <a href="#Ch37" title="Chapter 37 Architectural Adaptation (Parameter 𝜃 Learning) ‣ Part IX Learning and Adaptation ‣ Theoretical Foundations for Semantic Cognition in Artificial Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">37</span></a>), selecting appropriate architectural parameters (memory size <math id="A4.S4.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>, decay profiles <math id="A4.S4.I1.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{profile}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{profile}</annotation></semantics></math>, etc.) for a given application is crucial.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.S4.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.S4.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S4.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.S4.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Debugging and Validation:</span> Understanding and debugging the behavior of agents with complex internal dynamics and structures requires sophisticated logging, visualization (Chapter <a href="#Ch12" title="Chapter 12 Semantic Geometry ‣ Part III Structuring Belief: Modularity and Abstraction ‣ Theoretical Foundations for Semantic Cognition in Artificial Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>), and validation techniques.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.S5" class="ltx_section">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">D.5. </span>Conclusion</h4>

<div id="A4.S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Semantic Manifold framework offers a flexible blueprint adaptable to various implementation strategies, determined largely by the choice of representation mode <math id="A4.S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> within the architectural parameter vector <math id="A4.S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>. Symbolic, connectionist/embedding, and hybrid approaches each offer pathways for realizing the structured belief space <math id="A4.S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> and its core cognitive operators, with different trade-offs regarding interpretability, learning capacity, and formal precision. Large Language Models can serve as powerful components for specific operators within this broader architecture. The illustrative example highlights the dynamic interplay between assimilation, memory retrieval, and nullification in shaping belief over time. While practical implementation faces challenges, these strategies provide concrete starting points for building agents capable of the structured, dynamic, and regulated cognition envisioned by this framework.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Comparison with Related Cognitive Architectures and Frameworks</h3>

<section id="A5.S1" class="ltx_section">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">E.1. </span>Introduction</h4>

<div id="A5.S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Semantic Manifold framework presented in this monograph offers a distinct perspective on modeling structured belief and cognition. To better understand its positioning and potential contributions, this appendix compares its core tenets with several established cognitive architectures and related concepts in artificial intelligence. We focus on key dimensions such as representation, dynamics, learning, regulation, and overall architectural philosophy, drawing parallels and highlighting differences. This comparison aims to clarify the unique aspects of the Semantic Manifold approach, as summarized in Chapter <a href="#Ch41" title="Chapter 41 Conclusion ‣ Part XI Conclusion ‣ Theoretical Foundations for Semantic Cognition in Artificial Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">41</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.S2" class="ltx_section">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">E.2. </span>Comparison with Symbolic Cognitive Architectures (e.g., SOAR, ACT-R)</h4>

<div id="A5.S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Symbolic architectures like SOAR and ACT-R have significantly advanced our understanding of rule-based reasoning, procedural skill acquisition, and modeling human performance data.</p>
<ul id="A5.S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A5.S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Representation:</span> SOAR relies on production rules operating on working memory. ACT-R uses declarative memory chunks and production rules. The Semantic Manifold (<math id="A5.S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>) differs by positing a unified, linguistically-grounded belief space structured by geometry (<math id="A5.S2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma,k</annotation></semantics></math>), where states are ensembles <math id="A5.S2.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\varphi_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\varphi_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math> rather than primarily chunk- or rule-based. While chunks might map to anchored beliefs <math id="A5.S2.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, the emphasis on linguistic structure, continuous geometric notions (<math id="A5.S2.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d,F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d,F</annotation></semantics></math>), and explicit sectors is distinct.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dynamics:</span> Symbolic architectures focus on rule firing, chunk retrieval/activation dynamics, and conflict resolution. The Semantic Manifold introduces explicit operators for belief lifecycle (<math id="A5.S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A,N_{t},K,D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A,N_{t},K,D</annotation></semantics></math>) and navigation (<math id="A5.S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda,V,R,Q,M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda,V,R,Q,M</annotation></semantics></math>) operating directly on structured belief states, alongside potentially continuous flow dynamics (<math id="A5.S2.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>). <math id="A5.S2.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (gradual decay) differs significantly from simple chunk decay in ACT-R by incorporating anchoring <math id="A5.S2.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and structure sensitivity. Annihilation <math id="A5.S2.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> provides a more drastic reset mechanism.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Learning:</span> ACT-R has sophisticated mechanisms for learning chunks and tuning rule utilities. SOAR learns via chunking. The Semantic Manifold framework (Part <a href="#Pt9" title="Part IX Learning and Adaptation ‣ Theoretical Foundations for Semantic Cognition in Artificial Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IX</span></a>) accommodates learning at multiple levels (structures <math id="A5.S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d,\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d,\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>; operators <math id="A5.S2.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A,\Lambda,\dots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A,\Lambda,\dots</annotation></semantics></math>; policies <math id="A5.S2.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{effort},\pi_{regulate}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{effort},\pi_{regulate}</annotation></semantics></math>), potentially integrating RL and representation learning, but these are perhaps less developed currently than the learning theories in mature symbolic architectures.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.S2.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.S2.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.S2.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.S2.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Regulation:</span> While ACT-R includes base-level activation and utility calculations, the Semantic Manifold places stronger emphasis on explicit meta-cognitive regulation through dedicated mechanisms like Semantic Orientation (<math id="A5.S2.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta,r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta,r</annotation></semantics></math>), coherence monitoring (<math id="A5.S2.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>), load/effort management (<math id="A5.S2.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda,\epsilon,\pi_{effort}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda,\epsilon,\pi_{effort}</annotation></semantics></math>), and identity maintenance (<math id="A5.S2.I1.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math>), often operating within a reflective sector <math id="A5.S2.I1.i4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{refl}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{refl}</annotation></semantics></math> and using operator <math id="A5.S2.I1.i4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A5.S2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">In summary, the Semantic Manifold offers a more geometrically inspired, linguistically grounded space with a different suite of explicit dynamic operators and a stronger focus on intrinsic regulation compared to classic symbolic architectures.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.S3" class="ltx_section">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">E.3. </span>Comparison with Connectionist and Hybrid Architectures (e.g., CLARION)</h4>

<div id="A5.S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Connectionist approaches emphasize distributed representations and learning, while hybrid architectures like CLARION attempt to bridge symbolic and sub-symbolic processing.</p>
<ul id="A5.S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A5.S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Representation:</span> The Semantic Manifold allows for embedding-based representations (<math id="A5.S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>≈</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\approx</annotation></semantics></math> Embedding) or hybrid models (<math id="A5.S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>≈</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\approx</annotation></semantics></math> Hybrid), sharing similarities with connectionist and hybrid approaches. However, even with embeddings, the framework imposes explicit high-level structure (Sectors <math id="A5.S3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, Abstraction <math id="A5.S3.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{(k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{(k)}</annotation></semantics></math>) and assumes interpretable linguistic grounding (<math id="A5.S3.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi=\{\varphi_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi=\{\varphi_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math>) at some level, which may be less central in purely connectionist models. CLARION explicitly separates implicit (subsymbolic) and explicit (symbolic) knowledge; the Semantic Manifold integrates various belief types within the unified space <math id="A5.S3.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>, distinguishing them perhaps by sector (<math id="A5.S3.I1.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>) or abstraction (<math id="A5.S3.I1.i1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>) rather than architectural dichotomy.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Learning:</span> Connectionist models excel at learning from data. The Semantic Manifold framework incorporates learning (Part <a href="#Pt9" title="Part IX Learning and Adaptation ‣ Theoretical Foundations for Semantic Cognition in Artificial Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IX</span></a>) but emphasizes learning specific operators (<math id="A5.S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda,V,A,\dots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda,V,A,\dots</annotation></semantics></math>) and structures (<math id="A5.S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d,\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d,\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>) that align with its predefined architectural principles, potentially differing from end-to-end gradient descent across an entire network.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dynamics and Regulation:</span> The explicit dynamic operators (<math id="A5.S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{t},K,M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{t},K,M</annotation></semantics></math>, etc.) and regulatory mechanisms (Orientation, Gauge, Load/Effort) are defining features of the Semantic Manifold, providing structured control often absent or emergent in purely connectionist systems. CLARION includes drives and meta-cognitive processes, but the specific geometric regulation (Orientation) and belief lifecycle dynamics (<math id="A5.S3.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{t},K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{t},K</annotation></semantics></math>) proposed here are distinct.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A5.S3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The Semantic Manifold aims to integrate the learning power suggested by connectionism within a more explicitly structured and regulated cognitive architecture grounded in linguistic semantics.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.S4" class="ltx_section">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">E.4. </span>Comparison with Emergentist/Embodied Frameworks (e.g., Sigma, Active Inference)</h4>

<div id="A5.S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Frameworks like Sigma (focused on integrating graphical models and cognitive science principles) or Active Inference (based on Bayesian principles and free energy minimization) share interests in integration and grounding but differ architecturally.</p>
<ul id="A5.S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A5.S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Representation:</span> Sigma utilizes factor graphs and messages; Active Inference uses generative models and variational densities. The Semantic Manifold uses structured linguistic ensembles (<math id="A5.S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi=\{\varphi_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi=\{\varphi_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math>) within a geometric space (<math id="A5.S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi,\Sigma,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi,\Sigma,k</annotation></semantics></math>). While mappings might exist, the core representational formalisms differ.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dynamics:</span> Active Inference posits dynamics driven by minimizing prediction error (free energy). Sigma uses message passing. The Semantic Manifold defines explicit operators (<math id="A5.S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A,N_{t},K,\Lambda,V,M,R,Q,D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A,N_{t},K,\Lambda,V,M,R,Q,D</annotation></semantics></math>) with distinct functional roles related to belief construction, decay, abstraction, memory, and meta-cognition.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Regulation:</span> Active Inference frames regulation as part of the free energy minimization process (e.g., selecting actions or adjusting priors to minimize surprise). The Semantic Manifold proposes specific regulatory mechanisms tied to its geometry and structure (Orientation based on Null Tower axes, coherence <math id="A5.S4.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>, load <math id="A5.S4.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, effort <math id="A5.S4.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, identity <math id="A5.S4.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.S4.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.S4.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.S4.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.S4.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Emphasis:</span> The Semantic Manifold places strong emphasis on the internal structure and geometry of belief, the lifecycle dynamics (<math id="A5.S4.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{t},K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{t},K</annotation></semantics></math>), and explicit meta-cognitive operators (<math id="A5.S4.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>) and regulatory constructs (Orientation, Gauge, Identity), offering a different explanatory vocabulary compared to probabilistic inference or message passing frameworks.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A5.S4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">While sharing goals of unified cognition, the Semantic Manifold proposes a unique set of architectural primitives centered on geometric structure and explicit dynamic/regulatory operators over linguistic beliefs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.S5" class="ltx_section">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">E.5. </span>Relation to Modern AI Concepts (LLMs, Knowledge Graphs)</h4>

<div id="A5.S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is crucial to distinguish the Semantic Manifold framework from simply using components like Large Language Models (LLMs) or Knowledge Graphs (KGs).</p>
<ul id="A5.S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A5.S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Framework vs. Component:</span> LLMs offer powerful capabilities for processing and generating language; KGs provide structured knowledge storage. The Semantic Manifold is an architecture that can potentially leverage LLMs or KGs as implementation components (as discussed in Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Implementation Strategies and Examples ‣ Part XI Conclusion ‣ Theoretical Foundations for Semantic Cognition in Artificial Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>, e.g., for operators <math id="A5.S5.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A,V,R,Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A,V,R,Q</annotation></semantics></math> or for representing parts of <math id="A5.S5.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>) but provides an overarching structure and set of dynamics they inherently lack. An LLM alone is not typically considered a full cognitive architecture with persistent belief states (<math id="A5.S5.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>), explicit decay (<math id="A5.S5.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>), reflective regulation (<math id="A5.S5.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{refl},M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{refl},M</annotation></semantics></math>), or stable identity (<math id="A5.S5.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math>) in the way defined here.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Structured Dynamics and Regulation:</span> The Semantic Manifold defines specific operators (<math id="A5.S5.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{t},K,\Lambda,V,M,D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{t},K,\Lambda,V,M,D</annotation></semantics></math>) and regulatory mechanisms (<math id="A5.S5.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa,\lambda,\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa,\lambda,\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, Orientation, Gauge, Identity) that govern the evolution and stability of belief. These are generally absent from standard LLM or KG formalisms, which focus on generation/inference or knowledge representation, respectively.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.S5.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Parameterization (<math id="A5.S5.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{[\theta]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{[\theta]}</annotation></semantics></math>):</span> The framework allows specifying diverse architectures (<math id="A5.S5.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>) that might use LLMs/KGs internally but differ significantly in their designed memory (<math id="A5.S5.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>), decay (<math id="A5.S5.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{profile}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{profile}</annotation></semantics></math>), identity (<math id="A5.S5.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{type}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{type}</annotation></semantics></math>), etc.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A5.S5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The Semantic Manifold framework aims to provide the architectural context—the structured space, dynamics, and regulation—within which components like LLMs or KGs can be integrated to build more coherent, stable, and self-aware cognitive systems.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.S6" class="ltx_section">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">E.6. </span>Positioning and Unique Contributions</h4>

<div id="A5.S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Semantic Manifold framework, especially in its parameterized form <math id="A5.S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{[\theta]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{[\theta]}</annotation></semantics></math>, offers a synthesis and extension of previous approaches:</p>
<ul id="A5.S6.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A5.S6.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.S6.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.S6.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">From symbolic AI, it inherits compositional structure and the potential for interpretable reasoning.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.S6.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.S6.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.S6.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">From connectionism, it can adopt continuous representations (<math id="A5.S6.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>) and leverage powerful learning mechanisms for implementation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.S6.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.S6.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.S6.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">From predictive coding, it borrows concepts of hierarchical abstraction and internal regulation based on internal states.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.S6.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.S6.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.S6.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">From cognitive science, it integrates models of structured memory, introspection, coherence maintenance, and identity.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A5.S6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Its key novelty lies in the explicit formalization of cognition as a regulated trajectory (<math id="A5.S6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(t)</annotation></semantics></math>) within a structured, agent-specific semantic space (<math id="A5.S6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{[\theta]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{[\theta]}</annotation></semantics></math>), governed by defined operators (<math id="A5.S6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A,N_{t},K,\Lambda,V,M,R,Q,D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A,N_{t},K,\Lambda,V,M,R,Q,D</annotation></semantics></math>) and internal regulatory mechanisms (Orientation, Gauge, coherence <math id="A5.S6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>, load <math id="A5.S6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, effort <math id="A5.S6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, identity <math id="A5.S6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math>). It proposes a distinct cognitive architecture capable of coherence, drift, recovery, and self-alignment over time, providing a unique geometric and dynamic perspective on structured belief.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Extended Philosophical and Cognitive Motivations</h3>

<section id="A6.S1" class="ltx_section">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">F.1. </span>Introduction</h4>

<div id="A6.S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Chapters <a href="#Ch2" title="Chapter 2 Philosophical Motivations ‣ Part I Context and Foundations ‣ Theoretical Foundations for Semantic Cognition in Artificial Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, <a href="#Ch3" title="Chapter 3 Psychological Motivations ‣ Part I Context and Foundations ‣ Theoretical Foundations for Semantic Cognition in Artificial Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, and <a href="#Ch4" title="Chapter 4 Neuroscientific Motivations ‣ Part I Context and Foundations ‣ Theoretical Foundations for Semantic Cognition in Artificial Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> briefly outlined the grounding of the Semantic Manifold framework in various disciplines. This appendix offers optional, extended reflections on some of these foundational connections for readers interested in the deeper conceptual underpinnings. It does not introduce new core components but elaborates on the philosophical and cognitive context surrounding key architectural choices.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6.S2" class="ltx_section">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">F.2. </span>On the Linguistic Basis of Belief (<math id="A6.S2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi=\{\varphi_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi=\{\varphi_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math>)</h4>

<div id="A6.S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The framework’s central assumption that belief states <math id="A6.S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> are structured ensembles of linguistic expressions <math id="A6.S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> aligns with, but is not identical to, philosophical traditions like Fodor’s Language of Thought (LoT).</p>
<ul id="A6.S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A6.S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Compositionality and Productivity:</span> Like LoT, this assumption naturally accounts for the compositional nature of thought (complex beliefs built from simpler parts) and its productivity (the ability to generate novel beliefs). Representing beliefs linguistically provides a combinatorial syntax and semantics.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Interpretability:</span> Grounding <math id="A6.S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> in language makes internal states potentially interpretable, both for external observers/designers and for the agent itself via meta-cognitive processes (<math id="A6.S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, operating on <math id="A6.S2.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{refl}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{refl}</annotation></semantics></math>). This contrasts with purely sub-symbolic representations where meaning is often opaque.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Differences from Classical LoT:</span> Unlike some interpretations of LoT focusing on purely abstract, innate symbols, the <math id="A6.S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> here are framed as natural language expressions. Their meaning arises not just from formal structure but also from grounding (Chapter <a href="#Ch9" title="Chapter 9 Grounding Semantic Belief ‣ Part III Structuring Belief: Modularity and Abstraction ‣ Theoretical Foundations for Semantic Cognition in Artificial Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) via sensorimotor input (<math id="A6.S2.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>), linguistic context (<math id="A6.S2.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{text}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{text}</annotation></semantics></math> from corpora), simulation, and social interaction. Furthermore, the embedding within a dynamic, geometric manifold <math id="A6.S2.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> (governed by <math id="A6.S2.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A,N_{t},K,\Lambda,V,F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A,N_{t},K,\Lambda,V,F</annotation></semantics></math>, etc.) moves beyond the purely symbolic manipulation often associated with classical LoT.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.S2.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.S2.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.S2.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.S2.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scope Limitation?:</span> Does grounding belief in language limit the framework to language-using agents? While the current formalization emphasizes linguistic <math id="A6.S2.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, the underlying principles of a structured state space <math id="A6.S2.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>, operators, geometry, and regulation might potentially be generalized to non-linguistic representations (e.g., grounded sensorimotor schemas, affective states), though this remains a significant extension.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A6.S2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The linguistic basis provides a powerful blend of structure, interpretability, and generativity, serving as a productive, though not necessarily exclusive, foundation for modeling complex belief.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6.S3" class="ltx_section">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">F.3. </span>On Semantic Geometry and Conceptual Spaces</h4>

<div id="A6.S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The proposal to view <math id="A6.S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> geometrically (Chapter <a href="#Ch12" title="Chapter 12 Semantic Geometry ‣ Part III Structuring Belief: Modularity and Abstraction ‣ Theoretical Foundations for Semantic Cognition in Artificial Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>), structured by sectors <math id="A6.S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> and abstraction levels <math id="A6.S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{(k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{(k)}</annotation></semantics></math> and potentially endowed with a metric <math id="A6.S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, resonates with theories of conceptual spaces (e.g., Gardenfors).</p>
<ul id="A6.S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A6.S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Geometry as Meaning:</span> Conceptual space theories propose that concepts are represented as regions in multi-dimensional quality spaces, and meaning arises from geometric relationships (distance, convexity, neighborhood). Our Semantic Geometry shares this core idea, defining meaning partially through position <math id="A6.S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Sigma,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Sigma,k)</annotation></semantics></math> and proximity (<math id="A6.S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>) within <math id="A6.S3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Structure Beyond Quality Dimensions:</span> While conceptual spaces often focus on low-level perceptual dimensions, the Semantic Manifold’s geometry incorporates functional sectors (<math id="A6.S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>) and abstraction hierarchies (<math id="A6.S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{(k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{(k)}</annotation></semantics></math>) as primary organizing axes, providing structure relevant to higher-level cognition (planning, reflection, narrative) beyond direct perception.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dynamic Geometry:</span> Crucially, the Semantic Manifold emphasizes the dynamics within this space—belief evolution as trajectories <math id="A6.S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, governed by flow fields <math id="A6.S3.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> and operators <math id="A6.S3.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A,N_{t},K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A,N_{t},K</annotation></semantics></math>, etc. It is not just a static map but an active landscape. The metric <math id="A6.S3.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and even the sectoral structure <math id="A6.S3.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> might themselves be learned and adapted (Chapter <a href="#Ch34" title="Chapter 34 Learning Semantic Structures ‣ Part IX Learning and Adaptation ‣ Theoretical Foundations for Semantic Cognition in Artificial Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">34</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A6.S3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The geometric perspective provides a unifying language for structure and dynamics, connecting low-level representation to high-level reasoning through concepts of position, distance, and motion within the belief space.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6.S4" class="ltx_section">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">F.4. </span>On the Null Tower and Generative Abstraction</h4>

<div id="A6.S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Null Tower (Chapter <a href="#Ch6" title="Chapter 6 The Null Tower ‣ Part II Semantic Foundations: The Semantic Substrate ‣ Theoretical Foundations for Semantic Cognition in Artificial Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) offers a specific, generative account of how structured cognitive potential can emerge from the epistemic vacuum <math id="A6.S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
<ul id="A6.S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A6.S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Emergence from Potentiality:</span> Starting from irreducible null states <math id="A6.S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega\in\Omega^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega\in\Omega^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math> (representing pure potential or minimal architectural bias), the recursive application of abstraction <math id="A6.S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> constructs layered representations <math id="A6.S4.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{(k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{(k)}</annotation></semantics></math>. This models cognition building upon itself, generating complexity from minimal origins, echoing constructivist or developmental perspectives.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Abstraction as Compression and Form Extraction:</span> The <math id="A6.S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> operator is viewed not just as discarding information, but as extracting invariant structures or essential forms that persist across levels. The trajectory towards the singularity <math id="A6.S4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{(\infty)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{(\infty)}</annotation></semantics></math> represents a path towards pure semantic form, devoid of specific content but capturing the essence of the abstraction process itself.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Grounding Structure Internally:</span> Unlike approaches relying solely on external input (<math id="A6.S4.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>) to structure <math id="A6.S4.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>, the Null Tower provides an internal source of structure and orientation (via epistemic axes <math id="A6.S4.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[\omega\rightarrow\omega^{(\infty)}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega\rightarrow\omega^{(\infty)}]</annotation></semantics></math> used in Chapter <a href="#Ch22" title="Chapter 22 Semantic Orientation ‣ Part VI Regulation and Control: Compass, Gauge, Identity ‣ Theoretical Foundations for Semantic Cognition in Artificial Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a>), potentially explaining innate cognitive biases or foundational conceptual frameworks.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.S4.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.S4.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.S4.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.S4.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Relation to Mathematical Concepts:</span> The iterative process <math id="A6.S4.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{(k+1)}=\Lambda(\omega^{(k)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{(k+1)}=\Lambda(\omega^{(k)})</annotation></semantics></math> and the limit <math id="A6.S4.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{(\infty)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{(\infty)}</annotation></semantics></math> conceptually link to dynamical systems theory (fixed points, attractors) and potentially category theory (limits, colimits, functorial relationships between layers <math id="A6.S4.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{(k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{(k)}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="A6.S4.I1.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> has suitable properties).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A6.S4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The Null Tower provides a principled, though abstract, mechanism for the genesis of cognitive structure and internal reference frames.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6.S5" class="ltx_section">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">F.5. </span>On Gauge Equivalence and Functionalism</h4>

<div id="A6.S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Semantic Gauge Equivalence (<math id="A6.S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sim_{gauge}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim_{gauge}</annotation></semantics></math>, Chapter <a href="#Ch23" title="Chapter 23 Semantic Gauge ‣ Part VI Regulation and Control: Compass, Gauge, Identity ‣ Theoretical Foundations for Semantic Cognition in Artificial Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>) formally captures the idea that functional role, not specific representational form, is primary for certain aspects of cognition.</p>
<ul id="A6.S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A6.S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Alignment with Functionalism:</span> This resonates strongly with functionalist philosophies of mind, where mental states are defined by their causal relations to inputs, outputs, and other mental states, rather than their physical or structural realization. Gauge equivalence defines epistemic states by their behavior under the framework’s operators (<math id="A6.S5.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A,N_{t},K,\pi,\dots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A,N_{t},K,\pi,\dots</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Representational Flexibility:</span> It allows agents significant internal flexibility. They can reformat beliefs, use different phrasings <math id="A6.S5.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, or even different representational modes <math id="A6.S5.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> (to some extent), as long as functional equivalence is preserved at the level relevant for interaction and core dynamics defined by <math id="A6.S5.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.S5.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Identity and Continuity:</span> As discussed in Chapter <a href="#Ch24" title="Chapter 24 Epistemic Identity ‣ Part VI Regulation and Control: Compass, Gauge, Identity ‣ Theoretical Foundations for Semantic Cognition in Artificial Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a>, gauge theory suggests that epistemic identity <math id="A6.S5.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math> might persist even if the underlying belief structures <math id="A6.S5.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> change significantly, provided the agent remains within the same functional equivalence class <math id="A6.S5.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\phi]_{gauge}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\phi]_{gauge}</annotation></semantics></math> relevant to its identity-defining operations. Identity becomes functional continuity.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.S5.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.S5.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.S5.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.S5.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Limitations:</span> Gauge equivalence depends on the set of operators and contexts considered. Two states might be gauge-equivalent for simple operations but distinguishable by more sensitive introspective processes (<math id="A6.S5.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>) or finer-grained actions. The scope of <math id="A6.S5.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\approx_{behav}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>≈</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx_{behav}</annotation></semantics></math> in the definition is crucial.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A6.S5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Gauge theory provides a formal way to handle the "many-to-one" mapping between internal representation and external function.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6.S6" class="ltx_section">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">F.6. </span>On Memory, Dynamics, and Constructivism</h4>

<div id="A6.S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The framework’s approach to belief dynamics (Part <a href="#Pt4" title="Part IV Epistemic Dynamics: Evolution of Thought ‣ Theoretical Foundations for Semantic Cognition in Artificial Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a>) and memory (Part <a href="#Pt5" title="Part V Semantic Memory: Retrieval and Integration ‣ Theoretical Foundations for Semantic Cognition in Artificial Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V</span></a>) aligns with constructivist views of cognition.</p>
<ul id="A6.S6.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A6.S6.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.S6.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.S6.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.S6.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Active Construction (<math id="A6.S6.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>):</span> Assimilation is not passive storage but an active process of integrating input into existing structures <math id="A6.S6.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>, involving elaboration and potentially revision (<math id="A6.S6.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{corr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{corr}</annotation></semantics></math>), mirroring Bartlett’s ideas of memory as reconstruction based on schemas.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.S6.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.S6.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.S6.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.S6.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dynamic Memory:</span> Memory is not a static store but part of the dynamic space <math id="A6.S6.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>, subject to decay (<math id="A6.S6.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>) based on anchoring (<math id="A6.S6.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>) and reinforcement through retrieval and re-assimilation (<math id="A6.S6.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\rightarrow R\rightarrow A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\rightarrow R\rightarrow A</annotation></semantics></math>), aligning with usage-based theories of memory strength.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.S6.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.S6.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.S6.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.S6.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Forgetting as Functional (<math id="A6.S6.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{t},K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{t},K</annotation></semantics></math>):</span> Both gradual nullification (<math id="A6.S6.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>) and abrupt annihilation (<math id="A6.S6.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>) are presented not just as failures but as potentially functional aspects of managing cognitive load (<math id="A6.S6.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>) and maintaining coherence (<math id="A6.S6.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>), reflecting adaptive forgetting concepts in psychology.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.S6.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.S6.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.S6.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.S6.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Emergence from Dynamics:</span> Structures like stable beliefs (high <math id="A6.S6.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>), attractor states (stable points of <math id="A6.S6.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>), or even identity <math id="A6.S6.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math> emerge from the ongoing interplay of dynamic operators rather than being solely pre-specified.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A6.S6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This dynamic, constructivist perspective contrasts with models treating memory as a separate, static database or relying solely on retrieval strength without explicit decay/revision operators.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6.S7" class="ltx_section">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">F.7. </span>On Meta-Cognition and Self-Models</h4>

<div id="A6.S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The emphasis on meta-cognition (Part <a href="#Pt8" title="Part VIII Meta-Cognition and Recursive Regulation ‣ Theoretical Foundations for Semantic Cognition in Artificial Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VIII</span></a>: <math id="A6.S7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, Introspection, Trajectory Awareness, Load <math id="A6.S7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, Effort <math id="A6.S7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, Allocation <math id="A6.S7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{effort}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{effort}</annotation></semantics></math>) aligns with research highlighting the importance of self-monitoring and self-control for higher intelligence.</p>
<ul id="A6.S7.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A6.S7.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.S7.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.S7.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.S7.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Explicit Self-Representation (<math id="A6.S7.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{refl}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{refl}</annotation></semantics></math>):</span> Dedicating a sector to reflective processes and meta-beliefs allows for explicit reasoning about the self and internal states.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.S7.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.S7.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.S7.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.S7.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Internal Sensing (<math id="A6.S7.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{j}\rightarrow M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{j}\rightarrow M</annotation></semantics></math>):</span> Formalizing introspection as internal sensing feeding into Meta-Assimilation provides a mechanism for self-awareness to influence subsequent processing.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.S7.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.S7.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.S7.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.S7.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Resource Management (<math id="A6.S7.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda,\epsilon,\pi_{effort}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda,\epsilon,\pi_{effort}</annotation></semantics></math>):</span> Explicitly modeling load and effort acknowledges cognitive resource limits and enables the modeling of attentional control and strategic resource allocation, crucial aspects often abstracted away in simpler models.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.S7.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.S7.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.S7.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.S7.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Recursive Potential:</span> The framework naturally allows for recursion (beliefs about beliefs, monitoring of monitoring processes), supporting potentially deep levels of self-awareness, limited primarily by complexity and computational resources.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A6.S7.p1.2" class="ltx_p">By incorporating these meta-cognitive components explicitly, the framework aims to model agents capable of more robust, adaptive, and potentially insightful self-regulation compared to purely object-level reasoners.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
